                                {
                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "The Adjustment Sufficiency Problem",
                                    content: "When System 2 adjusts from an anchor, it stops too soon - not because it can't do the math, but because adjustment is mentally effortful. Researchers found:\n\n• People adjust about 50% of the distance from anchor to 'true value'\n• Even when you explicitly tell them the anchor is random, they still adjust insufficiently\n• The adjustment effort feels like 'enough' when it's nowhere near sufficient\n\n**Why**: System 1 suggests the anchored answer. System 2 does some token adjustment, then feels satisfied and stops. It's not error - it's premature satisfaction.",
                                    visual: {
                                        type: "diagram",
                                        svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" class="lesson-visual"><rect x="0" y="0" width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ec4899" font-weight="bold" font-size="42">THE ADJUSTMENT SUFFICIENCY PROBLEM</text><text x="550" y="120" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="26">We stop adjusting too soon because it's effortful</text><text x="100" y="190" fill="#888" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">ANCHOR: 10</text><text x="100" y="220" fill="#888" font-size="18">(We know it's random)</text><text x="100" y="380" fill="#888" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">TRUE VALUE: 50</text><text x="100" y="410" fill="#888" font-size="18">(Correct answer)</text><line x1="200" y1="200" x2="900" y2="200" stroke="#555" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="240" cy="200" r="12" fill="#ef4444" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2"/><text x="240" y="160" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-weight="bold" font-size="20">10</text><circle cx="900" cy="200" r="12" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="900" y="160" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold" font-size="20">50</text><path d="M240 200 L570 200" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="6" fill="none"/><circle cx="570" cy="200" r="15" fill="#f59e0b" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="2"/><text x="570" y="255" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-weight="bold" font-size="20">WHERE SYSTEM 2 STOPS</text><text x="570" y="285" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-size="18">~30 (halfway)</text><text x="570" y="320" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="18">Adjustment feels "done"</text><rect x="60" y="380" width="980" height="100" rx="15" fill="rgba(245,158,11,0.15)" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="2"/><text x="550" y="415" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-weight="bold" font-size="24">ADJUSTMENT STOPS 50% OF THE WAY</text><text x="550" y="450" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">System 2 feels satisfied, stops adjusting, moves on</text></svg>`
                                    }
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "fact",
                                    title: "Anchoring Works Better With Numbers",
                                    content: "Anchoring is particularly powerful with numerical estimates. It works less well when:\n• The estimate is qualitative (colors, shapes)\n• The person is an expert in the domain\n• Multiple anchors conflict (creating confusion)\n\nBut it works better when:\n• You're estimating quantities (distances, prices, weights)\n• You don't have strong prior knowledge\n• The anchor is presented confidently\n\n**This is why skilled negotiators always anchor first with a specific number, not a range or vague estimate.**",
                                    visual: {
                                        type: "diagram",
                                        svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" class="lesson-visual"><rect x="0" y="0" width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="80" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f59e0b" font-weight="bold" font-size="42">WHEN ANCHORING WORKS BEST</text><text x="550" y="120" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="26">Specific numbers are most powerful anchors</text><rect x="60" y="170" width="470" height="320" rx="20" fill="rgba(239,68,68,0.12)" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="3"/><text x="295" y="220" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ef4444" font-weight="bold" font-size="26">WORKS WELL</text><text x="295" y="270" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Numerical estimates</text><text x="295" y="300" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">(prices, distances, weights)</text><text x="295" y="345" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Low expertise</text><text x="295" y="375" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">(Don't know much about domain)</text><text x="295" y="420" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Confident presentation</text><text x="295" y="450" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">("Definitely $2M")</text><rect x="570" y="170" width="470" height="320" rx="20" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.12)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="3"/><text x="805" y="220" text-anchor="middle" fill="#10b981" font-weight="bold" font-size="26">WORKS LESS WELL</text><text x="805" y="270" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Qualitative estimates</text><text x="805" y="300" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">(colors, shapes, qualities)</text><text x="805" y="345" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">High expertise</text><text x="805" y="375" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">(Strong domain knowledge)</text><text x="805" y="420" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="18">Conflicting anchors</text><text x="805" y="450" text-anchor="middle" fill="#888" font-size="16">(Multiple numbers confuse)</text><rect x="150" y="530" width="800" height="120" rx="20" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.15)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="3"/><text x="550" y="575" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8b5cf6" font-weight="bold" font-size="26">PRO TIP: SPECIFIC NUMBERS</text><text x="550" y="620" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="20">Say "$4,850" not "$5,000" - specific numbers anchor more strongly</text></svg>`
                                    }
                                }
