                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Wabi-Sabi as Antidote to Perfectionism",
                                    content: "Modern culture bombards you with messages about optimization: the perfect body, the perfect career, the perfect morning routine.\n\n**Wabi-sabi offers a radical counter-message**: Stop. You are enough as you are — imperfect, unfinished, and changing.\n\nSen no Rikyu built his tea rooms with deliberately rough walls and uneven floors. He chose wildflowers over arranged bouquets. He served tea in bowls that a factory would reject.\n\n**His message**: The pursuit of perfection is a kind of violence against yourself. Beauty emerges when you stop trying to control everything and let things be what they are.",
