                                    type: "intro",
                                    title: "Why Does Saying 'Itadakimasu' Change a Meal?",
                                    content: "Before every meal in Japan, people say **itadakimasu** — 'I humbly receive.' It's not a prayer exactly. It's an acknowledgment: of the food, the hands that prepared it, the life that was given. This small ritual transforms eating from consumption into **communion**. And this is the power of ritual — it doesn't change what you're doing, it changes **how you experience** what you're doing. Paired with **ma** — the Japanese concept of meaningful pause and negative space — ritual becomes a technology for presence.",
