                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Happiness Isn't Pursued — It's Received",
                                    content: "This is perhaps the most important insight from the entire book: **you don't chase happiness. You create the conditions to receive it.**\n\nWestern culture says: 'Set goals. Achieve them. Then you'll be happy.'\n\nJapanese wisdom says: 'Pay attention. Be present. Happiness is already here.'\n\nThis doesn't mean you stop working or stop growing. It means you stop **postponing** your experience of life. The ikigai is in this morning's coffee. The wabi-sabi is in your imperfect apartment. The shinrin-yoku is in the tree outside your window. The mono no aware is in this very breath — which will never come again.\n\n**You don't need to go to Japan to find these eight million paths. You need to go to this moment.**",
