        content: "When Melchizedek gives Santiago the two flat stones — one black, one white — he calls them **Urim and Thummim**.\n\nThese are real objects from ancient Hebrew tradition, used by the High Priest to discern God's will. Black meant no; white meant yes.\n\nIn the novel, they serve a specific function: **for absolute yes/no questions when the heart cannot decide alone.**\n\nBut here's the crucial twist: Melchizedek tells Santiago that the stones will become unnecessary over time.\n\n*\"Usually the answers come from your own heart. The stones are just a way of asking questions until you learn to trust yourself.\"*\n\nThe real lesson: **The stones are training wheels for intuition.** The goal is not to rely on external tools forever — it's to develop the internal capacity to read omens directly.",
