        content: "You've seen this pattern — maybe even lived it.\n\n**The job seeker** who resigns from a job they hate, feeling terrified — and then gets an unsolicited call from a recruiter the next morning. Did the universe conspire? Or did their energy shift, they updated their LinkedIn, and they mentioned it to a friend at dinner?\n\n**The writer** who finally submits the manuscript they've been sitting on for two years — and gets a response within a week. Was it fate? Or did the manuscript improve with every rewrite, and submission is simply something the universe rewards with probability?\n\nCoelho wouldn't distinguish between these. His point: **when you act on your legend, things happen that wouldn't have happened if you'd stayed put.**\n\nThe early wins are real. They are meant to be *noticed and remembered* — because later, when you are sitting in your equivalent of Tangier with nothing, you will need to recall: *The path was real. I have proof.*",
