        content: "Santiago arrives in Tangier — his first stop on the way to Egypt. He finds a young man who speaks Spanish, who offers to guide him. The man takes his money and disappears.\n\nIn one moment, Santiago loses everything: his money, his connection, his plan. He doesn't speak Arabic. He knows no one. He is completely alone in a foreign city.\n\nThis is the **test.**\n\nCoelho's framing is precise: this is not punishment. This is not proof the dream was foolish. This is the universe asking, with complete seriousness:\n\n**\"Do you really want this? Or were you only willing to pursue it when it was easy?\"**\n\nEvery great quest has this moment. The mystic traditions call it the Dark Night of the Soul. Joseph Campbell called it the Ordeal. John of the Cross wrote a 16th-century poem about it. The hero must face the abyss — the moment when all external support disappears and only interior conviction remains.\n\nWhat you do in that moment determines everything.",
