            content: "When Santiago finally reaches the pyramids and begins to dig, a group of thieves find him and beat him. One of them — mocking him — says:\n\n*'I once had a dream too. Treasure buried under a ruined church in Spain, beneath a sycamore tree. But I'm not stupid enough to cross the world for a dream.'*\n\n**Santiago immediately recognizes the place.** It's the ruined church in Andalusia where he slept with his sheep at the very beginning of the story.\n\nThe treasure was there all along. A thief learned of it in a dream and laughed it off. Santiago, by contrast, crossed deserts, survived robbery, learned alchemy, became the wind — and is now the only person on earth who can go back to that church and know with absolute certainty where to dig.\n\n**The journey was not the path to the treasure. It was the making of the person capable of receiving it.**\n\nCoelho's greatest teaching is hidden in this twist: you had to become the version of yourself worthy of the dream before the dream could be yours.",
