                                {
                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "The Ego Trap in Ramadi",
                                    content: "A SEAL platoon had an operator who was exceptionally skilled but had an ego problem. He constantly questioned decisions, undermined other team members, and believed he knew better than everyone.\n\n**The Impact:**\n• Team cohesion suffered\n• Other operators felt devalued\n• Information sharing decreased\n• Mission effectiveness dropped\n\n**The Resolution:**\nLeadership had a direct conversation. The choice was clear: check the ego or leave. Even exceptional talent isn't worth team destruction."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "quiz",
                                    question: "What's the key difference between confidence and ego?",
                                    options: [
                                        { text: "Confidence is weak, ego is strong", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Confidence welcomes feedback and learning; ego is defensive and closed", correct: true },
                                        { text: "They're the same thing", correct: false },
                                        { text: "Ego is necessary for leadership", correct: false }
                                    ],
                                    explanation: "Healthy confidence allows leaders to remain open to feedback, admit mistakes, and continue learning. Ego creates defensiveness, prevents growth, and undermines team dynamics. Leaders need confidence without ego."
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "application",
                                    title: "Ego Check Questions",
                                    content: "**Ask yourself regularly:**\n\n• Am I getting defensive when criticized?\n• Do I need to be right more than I need to find the truth?\n• Am I taking credit that belongs to others?\n• Am I dismissing ideas because of who suggested them?\n• Would I rather look good or actually succeed?\n• When was the last time I said 'I was wrong'?\n\n**If you can't remember the last time you admitted a mistake, your ego needs checking.**"
                                },
                                {
                                    type: "takeaway",
                                    title: "Key Insight",
                                    content: "Ego is the invisible enemy that clouds judgment and destroys teams from within. The most dangerous aspect of ego is that those with the biggest egos are least likely to recognize it. Make ego-checking a constant practice: seek criticism, admit mistakes openly, give credit away, and always prioritize the mission over personal glory."
                                }
