                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Life on Earth: Extreme Survivors",
                                    content: "Studying life on Earth has expanded our understanding of where life might exist:\n\n**Extremophiles**—organisms thriving in conditions once thought impossible:\n\n• **Thermophiles**: Living in boiling hot springs (up to 122°C)\n• **Psychrophiles**: Thriving in Antarctic ice (-20°C)\n• **Acidophiles**: Living in pH 0 environments (like battery acid)\n• **Halophiles**: Surviving in salt concentrations that kill other organisms\n• **Radioresistant**: Deinococcus radiodurans survives 3,000× the radiation that kills humans\n• **Piezophiles**: Thriving at crushing deep-sea pressures\n\n**Implications:**\n• Life is far more adaptable than we once thought\n• The habitable zone may be much wider than traditional definitions\n• Subsurface oceans on icy moons become plausible habitats\n• Even Mars's harsh conditions might support microbial life\n\n**LUCA** (Last Universal Common Ancestor) likely lived near hydrothermal vents—life may have originated in extreme environments, not temperate ones."
