                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Father Feynman: The First Teacher",
                                    content: "Feynman's father, Melville, had no scientific training—he sold uniforms. But he taught Richard something invaluable: **the difference between knowing the name of something and actually understanding it.**\n\n**The Bird Story:**\nOne day, young Richard asked his father: 'What's that bird called?'\n\nHis father replied: 'That's a brown-throated thrush. In Portuguese, they call it Bom da Peida. In Italian, it's Chutto Lapittida. In Chinese, it's Chung-long-tah. Now you know the bird's name in every language, but you still know absolutely nothing about the bird.'\n\nThen he showed Richard what the bird was DOING—how it pecked, why it pecked, what it was looking for.\n\n**The revelation:** Names are labels. Understanding is power. You can memorize every term in a textbook and understand nothing.",
