                                    type: "intro",
                                    title: "The Nobel Prize Winner Who Cracked Safes",
                                    content: "During World War II, Richard Feynman worked on the Manhattan Project at Los Alamos—the secret laboratory building the atomic bomb. Surrounded by the most sensitive documents in human history, protected by heavy safes with combination locks, one man became legendary.\n\nNot for physics. For **cracking safes.**\n\nFeynman didn't have special tools or insider knowledge. He had something better: a systematic way of thinking that could defeat any system designed by humans. By the end of the war, he could open most safes on the base—and the generals were NOT happy about it.\n\nThis lesson isn't about becoming a safecracker. It's about learning to think from **first principles**—breaking any problem down to its fundamental elements and building understanding from there.",
