                                    title: "The Man Who Had to Touch Everything",
                                    content: "Feynman couldn't just read about something—he had to DO it. He didn't study locks from textbooks; he picked them. He didn't learn biology from lectures; he borrowed a lab and started cutting up cells.\n\nWhen he wanted to understand art, he didn't read critics—he started drawing. When curious about ants, he didn't read research—he spent hours following them around his house with sugar.\n\nThis wasn't just a quirk. It was a fundamental philosophy: **True understanding comes from active engagement, not passive absorption.**\n\nThe world is full of people who read about things. Feynman was one of the rare people who actually did them—and that made all the difference.",
