                                    title: "The Resting Membrane Potential",
                                    content: "When a neuron is at rest (not firing), it maintains a **resting membrane potential** of about **-70 mV**—the inside is negative relative to outside.\n\n**Why the negative charge?**\n1. **Unequal ion distribution:**\n   • More Kâº (potassium) INSIDE the cell\n   • More Naâº (sodium) OUTSIDE the cell\n   • Large negative proteins stuck inside\n\n2. **The Sodium-Potassium Pump (Naâº/Kâº-ATPase):**\n   • Actively pumps 3 Naâº OUT and 2 Kâº IN\n   • Uses ATP energy (20-40% of brain's energy!)\n   • Creates concentration gradients\n\n3. **Leak channels:**\n   • More Kâº leak channels than Naâº\n   • Kâº flows out, leaving negative charge behind\n\n**Result:** The neuron is \"polarized\"—charged like a tiny battery, ready to fire."
