                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "All-or-Nothing: The Digital Code",
                                    content: "Action potentials follow the **all-or-nothing principle:**\n\n• **Subthreshold stimulus:** No action potential (signal dies out)\n• **Threshold reached (-55 mV):** FULL action potential fires\n• Once triggered, always the SAME size (~110 mV change)\n\n**How do we encode intensity then?**\n\n**Frequency coding:**\n• Stronger stimuli â†’ MORE action potentials per second\n• Weak touch: 10 Hz (10 spikes/second)\n• Strong pain: 100+ Hz (100 spikes/second)\n\n**Population coding:**\n• Stronger stimuli â†’ MORE neurons firing\n• Recruit additional neurons for intensity\n\n**Comparison to computers:**\n• Digital: Binary (0s and 1s)\n• Neurons: Binary spikes, but RATE carries information\n• Your brain: ~86 billion processing units, massively parallel"
