                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Capillary Exchange Mechanisms",
                                    content: "Capillaries are where the real work happens—exchange between blood and tissues:\n\n**Types of Capillaries:**\n• **Continuous** - Tight junctions, BBB, muscle, skin\n• **Fenestrated** - Windows/pores, kidneys, intestines, endocrine glands\n• **Sinusoidal** - Large gaps, liver, spleen, bone marrow\n\n**Exchange Mechanisms:**\n\n**1. Diffusion** - Oâ‚‚, COâ‚‚, small lipid-soluble molecules\n**2. Filtration** - Water and small solutes pushed out by hydrostatic pressure\n**3. Osmosis** - Water pulled back in by oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure\n**4. Transcytosis** - Large molecules transported via vesicles\n\n**Starling Forces:**\n• Hydrostatic pressure pushes OUT (arterial end)\n• Oncotic pressure pulls IN (venous end)\n• Net filtration at arterial end, net absorption at venous end"
