                                    explanation: "The SAID principle (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands) states that the body adapts specifically to the type of stress placed upon it. A cyclist would develop cycling-specific adaptations—leg endurance, cycling economy, and cardiovascular fitness in the cycling position—but these wouldn't transfer fully to running. This is why cross-training helps overall fitness but sport-specific training is essential for competition."
