                                    type: "concept",
                                    title: "Common Evidence Manipulation Tactics",
                                    content: "Watch for these ways evidence gets distorted:\n\n**Cherry-Picking**\nSelecting only evidence that supports the conclusion while ignoring contradicting data.\n*\"9 out of 10 dentists...\" (Which dentists? How selected?)*\n\n**Base Rate Neglect**\nFocusing on a percentage without context.\n*\"50% increase in risk!\" (From 1 in 10 million to 1.5 in 10 million)*\n\n**Misleading Graphs**\nManipulating visual representation to exaggerate trends.\n*Truncated axes, inconsistent scales*\n\n**Selection Bias**\nStudying a non-representative sample.\n*Internet surveys only reach internet users*\n\n**P-Hacking**\nRunning many tests until something appears \"statistically significant\" by chance.",
