                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Statutes of Limitations & Damage Caps",
                                    content: "**Statute of Limitations** sets a deadline for filing a lawsuit. In medical malpractice:\n\n**Typical timeframes:** 2-3 years from the date of injury (varies by state)\n\n**The Discovery Rule:** The clock doesn't start until the patient **knew or should have known** about the injury. Example: A sponge left in a patient during surgery isn't discovered for 2 years — the statute starts running at discovery, not the surgery date.\n\n**Tolling for Minors:** Most states extend the deadline for children. Some states allow filing until the child reaches the **age of majority plus** the standard limitation period. This is why **pediatric records must be retained much longer** than adult records.\n\n**Damage Caps:**\nMany states limit the amount of **non-economic damages** (pain and suffering) that can be awarded in malpractice cases:\n• California (MICRA): Was $250,000, increased to $350,000-$750,000 (2023 update)\n• Texas: $250,000 per physician, $500,000 cap per healthcare institution\n• Some states have NO cap\n\n**HIM Connection:** Record retention schedules are directly tied to statutes of limitations. You must retain records long enough for potential litigation to be filed.",
