                                    explanation: "Res ipsa loquitur ('the thing speaks for itself') is the classic doctrine for retained surgical instruments. It applies when: (1) the injury wouldn't normally occur without negligence, (2) the instrument was under the defendant's exclusive control, and (3) the patient didn't contribute. A sponge inside a patient meets all three criteria — it shifts the burden to the defendant to explain how it happened. While respondeat superior and corporate negligence may also apply, res ipsa loquitur is the signature doctrine for this exact scenario.",
