                                    content: "**As an HIM professional, you manage the records that prove consent was obtained. Here's what to do:**\n\n1. **Verify consent forms are complete** — Check that all five elements are addressed, the form is signed and dated by the patient (or authorized representative), and a witness signature is present where required.\n\n2. **Check for capacity documentation** — If there's any question about the patient's ability to consent, the physician should document a capacity assessment. Flag missing capacity documentation.\n\n3. **Know your state's minor consent laws** — These determine which records parents can access. When a minor consents independently, parental access may be restricted.\n\n4. **Document advance directives** — Flag the presence (or absence) of advance directives in the medical record per your facility's policy. The PSDA requires this at admission.\n\n5. **Handle consent revocation properly** — Patients can revoke consent at any time. When they do, document it immediately and ensure the care team is notified. A signed revocation should be placed in the record.",
