                                    content: "**Scenario: A patient files a malpractice lawsuit**\n\nThe moment your organization receives notice of a lawsuit (or reasonably anticipates one), a **litigation hold** goes into effect. Here's what happens:\n\n**Immediate actions:**\n• ALL relevant records are **frozen** — no destruction, no modification, no routine purging\n• IT department preserves electronic systems, backups, and audit logs\n• The legal hold applies to **all formats**: paper charts, EHR entries, emails, text messages, voicemails, images, billing records\n• Staff are notified not to delete or alter anything related to the case\n\n**E-Discovery obligations:**\nUnder modern litigation rules, **electronically stored information (ESI)** is discoverable. This includes:\n• EHR entries and audit trails (who accessed what, when)\n• **Metadata** — the hidden data behind documents (creation date, modification history, author)\n• Emails between providers about the patient\n• System configuration records (were alerts functioning?)\n\n**HIM's role:**\n• Identify and preserve all relevant records\n• Coordinate with legal counsel on the scope of the hold\n• Ensure the EHR audit trail is preserved\n• Suspend any scheduled destruction of records within scope\n\n**Failure to preserve = spoliation sanctions**: fines, adverse inference instructions, or even default judgment.",
