                                    type: "example",
                                    title: "Right of Access in Practice: OCR Enforcement Cases",
                                    content: "OCR's **Right of Access Initiative** has resulted in dozens of enforcement actions:\n\n**Case 1: Bayfront Health St. Petersburg ($85,000)**\nA patient requested medical records. The hospital took over 9 months to provide them, far exceeding the 30-day requirement. OCR's investigation found systemic failures in the facility's access process.\n\n**Case 2: Banner Health ($200,000)**\nA patient requested a copy of their records. The hospital failed to provide timely access despite multiple requests. OCR found the failure was not isolated — multiple patients experienced similar delays.\n\n**Case 3: Dr. Rajendra Bhatt ($15,000)**\nA small medical practice failed to provide a patient with records after repeated requests. Even solo practitioners are subject to the right of access.\n\n**Common violations:**\n• Exceeding the 30-day timeline\n• Charging excessive fees (retrieval fees, per-page fees above cost-based amounts)\n• Requiring patients to appear in person to request records\n• Refusing to send records to third parties designated by the patient\n• Failing to provide records in the requested format when readily producible",
