                                    type: "application",
                                    title: "HIM Scenario: Multi-Law Privacy Analysis",
                                    content: "**Scenario:** You are the HIM Director at a hospital that includes a federally assisted substance abuse treatment program. A patient, Maria, was treated for opioid use disorder in your SUD program, tested positive for HIV during treatment, and has a history of bipolar disorder documented in the general medical record. Maria's employer requests her \"complete medical records\" with a signed general authorization.\n\n**Analysis — Three overlapping protections:**\n\n**1. Substance Abuse Records (42 CFR Part 2):**\n• Maria's SUD treatment records require **specific Part 2 consent** — not a general authorization\n• The employer's general authorization is **insufficient**\n• Must contain all 10 required elements and redisclosure prohibition notice\n\n**2. HIV Status (State HIV Confidentiality Law):**\n• Most states require **HIV-specific written consent** separate from general authorization\n• Some states prohibit disclosure to employers entirely\n• Must check your **state's specific requirements**\n\n**3. Mental Health Records (HIPAA + State Law):**\n• If psychotherapy notes exist → **always** requires authorization (cannot use general auth)\n• General mental health records → check if state law is more restrictive than HIPAA\n\n**Correct Action:** Deny release based on insufficient authorization. Contact Maria to explain she needs separate, specific consents for each category. Release only what is properly authorized.",
