            type: "example",
            title: "Example: Interprofessional Team",
            content: "**Scenario:** Mrs. Johnson, 68, is admitted after a stroke. She has right-sided weakness, difficulty swallowing, and new-onset diabetes discovered during hospitalization.\n\n**The Interprofessional Team:**\n\n• **Neurologist (MD)** — Leads stroke treatment, orders tPA if within window, manages medical plan\n• **Hospitalist (MD/DO)** — Manages day-to-day inpatient care and comorbidities\n• **RN (BSN)** — 24-hour bedside care, medication administration, assessment, patient education, care coordination\n• **Physical Therapist (DPT)** — Evaluates mobility, develops exercise program, works on gait and transfers\n• **Occupational Therapist (OT)** — Helps relearn ADLs (dressing, bathing, eating with left hand)\n• **Speech-Language Pathologist (SLP)** — Swallowing evaluation (dysphagia), communication therapy\n• **Registered Dietitian (RDN)** — Modified diet for dysphagia + diabetes meal planning, medical nutrition therapy\n• **Pharmacist (PharmD)** — Medication reconciliation, anticoagulation management, drug interaction screening\n• **Social Worker (MSW/LCSW)** — Discharge planning, insurance navigation, emotional support, rehab facility placement\n• **HIM Professional (RHIA/CCS)** — Ensures accurate coding (ICD-10-CM stroke code, MS-DRG assignment), CDI queries, documentation supports medical necessity\n\n**Key Principle:** Collaborative, patient-centered care improves outcomes. Each professional brings unique expertise. Communication (SBAR, huddles, interdisciplinary rounds) is the glue.",
