            content: "Private health insurance covers approximately **65% of the U.S. population** — about 214 million people. Most Americans with private insurance obtain it through their **employer** (employer-sponsored insurance, or ESI), while a growing number purchase individual plans through **ACA marketplaces**.\n\nThis lesson covers the fundamental building blocks of health insurance — premiums, deductibles, copays, coinsurance — and the major plan types (HMO, PPO, EPO, POS) that define how patients access care and how providers get paid.\n\n**Managed care** revolutionized healthcare delivery starting in the 1970s–1980s by introducing cost-containment strategies: **gatekeeping**, **utilization management**, **network restrictions**, and **capitation**. Today, the vast majority of private insurance plans incorporate some form of managed care.\n\nFor HIM professionals, understanding insurance mechanics is critical because:\n• **Pre-authorization requirements** affect documentation timelines\n• **Plan type** determines referral and network rules that impact claims\n• **Utilization review** decisions affect patient length of stay and care transitions\n• **Value-based contracts** with payers require quality data that HIM manages",
