            content: "**Scenario:** Mrs. Johnson, age 72, is admitted to the hospital for a hip replacement.\n\n**Part A covers her inpatient stay:**\n• Hospital room (semi-private)\n• Nursing care, meals, operating room\n• Inpatient drugs, lab work, anesthesia\n• She pays the **Part A deductible ($1,632)** and $0 coinsurance for days 1-60\n\n**After discharge, Part B kicks in:**\n• Follow-up visits with her orthopedic surgeon — **Part B**\n• Outpatient physical therapy — **Part B**\n• Walker (DME) — **Part B** (80/20 split after $240 deductible)\n• Blood work at the lab — **Part B** (clinical lab services often 100% covered)\n\n**Key Distinction for HIM:**\nThe **setting of care** determines A vs B. The same service (like a CT scan) is Part A if performed during an inpatient stay, but Part B if performed in an outpatient or physician office setting. This matters enormously for **coding and billing** — inpatient claims go on a **UB-04 (CMS-1450)** form; physician/outpatient professional claims go on a **CMS-1500** form.\n\n**Observation status trap:** If Mrs. Johnson had been placed on **observation status** instead of admitted, her entire stay would be billed under **Part B outpatient**, even though she occupies a hospital bed. This has major cost implications for patients.",
