            content: "**Scenario:** Mr. Rivera, age 68, has limited income and qualifies for both Medicare and Medicaid. He is a **dual-eligible** beneficiary.\n\n**What Does Dual-Eligible Mean?**\nApproximately **12 million Americans** are enrolled in both Medicare and Medicaid simultaneously. These individuals are among the most complex and costly patients in the U.S. healthcare system.\n\n**How Coverage Works:**\n• **Medicare is the primary payer** — it pays first for Medicare-covered services\n• **Medicaid is the secondary payer** — it picks up remaining costs (deductibles, copayments, coinsurance) that Medicare does not cover\n• Medicaid may also cover services Medicare does not — such as **long-term care, dental, vision, hearing, and transportation**\n\n**Mr. Rivera's Hip Replacement:**\n• Hospital inpatient stay: **Medicare Part A** pays first (minus $1,632 deductible)\n• Medicaid pays the Part A deductible for Mr. Rivera (he cannot afford it)\n• Post-discharge physician visits: **Medicare Part B** pays 80%\n• Medicaid pays the remaining 20% copayment\n• Prescription drugs: covered under **Medicare Part D** (low-income subsidy may eliminate premiums and reduce copays)\n• Dental cleaning: **Medicaid** covers this (Medicare does not)\n\n**HIM Relevance:** Dual-eligible claims require coordination of benefits (COB). The hospital must bill Medicare first, then bill Medicaid for the remaining balance. Incorrect billing order = claim denial.",
