            content: "**Indian Health Service (IHS):**\n• A federal agency within **HHS** that provides healthcare to approximately **2.6 million American Indians and Alaska Natives**\n• Operates under a **government-to-government relationship** based on treaty obligations\n• Services delivered through three systems: **IHS-operated facilities, tribally-operated programs (under Self-Determination Act), and Urban Indian Health Programs** — collectively called the **I/T/U system**\n• IHS is **chronically underfunded** — per capita spending is significantly less than other federal health programs\n• IHS facilities include hospitals, health centers, health stations, and school health centers across **37 states**\n• Eligible individuals can also have Medicare, Medicaid, or private insurance — IHS can bill these payers\n\n**Workers' Compensation:**\n• **State-mandated insurance** that covers employees for **work-related injuries and illnesses**\n• Each state has its own Workers' Compensation laws and system\n• **Employers pay the premiums** — employees pay nothing\n• Benefits include: medical care, temporary/permanent disability payments, vocational rehabilitation, death benefits\n• Workers' Comp is a **no-fault system** — the employee does not need to prove employer negligence\n• In exchange, employees generally give up the right to sue the employer for workplace injuries\n• **HIM relevance:** Workers' Comp claims require specific documentation — date of injury, mechanism of injury, body part affected, causal relationship to employment\n• Workers' Comp has its own **fee schedules** and may use different coding guidelines than Medicare/commercial payers",
