            explanation: "The primary driver was cost control. Under the retrospective system, hospitals had no incentive to be efficient — they billed for every service and every day, and Medicare paid. Costs were rising unsustainably. TEFRA (1982) mandated a prospective system where payment is predetermined by diagnosis (DRG). If the hospital can treat the patient for less than the DRG payment, they keep the surplus. If it costs more, they absorb the loss. This created powerful incentives for efficiency. While quality and simplification were secondary considerations, cost containment was the driving force.",
