            content: "**Scenario:** A coder reviews the record of a 74-year-old patient admitted for pneumonia. The physician documented:\n• Community-acquired pneumonia\n• Type 2 diabetes (on insulin)\n• Acute respiratory failure\n• History of COPD\n\n**Coder A (Experienced):**\nCaptures all diagnoses:\n• Principal Dx: J18.9 (Pneumonia, unspecified organism)\n• Secondary: J96.00 (Acute respiratory failure — **MCC**)\n• Secondary: E11.65 (Type 2 DM with hyperglycemia — **CC**)\n• Secondary: J44.1 (COPD with acute exacerbation — **CC**)\n• **Result: MS-DRG 177 (Respiratory Infections with MCC) — RW 1.4145 — Payment ~$9,200**\n\n**Coder B (Misses the MCC):**\nMisses acute respiratory failure:\n• Principal Dx: J18.9 (Pneumonia)\n• Secondary: E11.65 (Type 2 DM — CC)\n• Secondary: J44.1 (COPD — CC)\n• **Result: MS-DRG 178 (Respiratory Infections with CC) — RW 0.9461 — Payment ~$6,100**\n\n**Revenue Impact:**\n• Difference per case: **$3,100**\n• If this pattern occurs 100 times per year: **$310,000 in lost revenue**\n• This is why CC/MCC capture rates are tracked as a key HIM metric\n\nThis is not upcoding — the respiratory failure WAS documented. Missing it is **undercoding**, which is equally problematic because it misrepresents the patient's severity and the resources used.",
