            content: "Every hospital, nursing home, and healthcare facility in the United States operates under layers of oversight — **federal regulations, state licensing, and voluntary accreditation**. This system exists to protect patients and ensure a minimum standard of care.\n\nBut who enforces these standards? How do they differ? And what happens when a facility fails to meet them?\n\nAs an HIM professional, you will interact with these regulatory and accreditation bodies constantly. When **The Joint Commission** conducts an unannounced survey, your department's documentation practices are scrutinized. When **CMS** updates its **Conditions of Participation**, your facility must comply or risk losing Medicare reimbursement. When the **OIG** flags a billing pattern, your coding accuracy is under the microscope.\n\nThis lesson maps the entire regulatory landscape — from federal CMS requirements to state licensing to private accreditation organizations. Understanding who has authority over what, and how **deemed status** connects accreditation to Medicare participation, is essential knowledge for every HIM professional.\n\nThe stakes are real: **a facility that loses CMS certification cannot bill Medicare or Medicaid** — which for most hospitals would mean financial collapse.",
