            type: "concept",
            title: "Professional Certification: Voluntary Excellence",
            content: "**Certification** is a **voluntary** process by which a **professional organization** (not the government) recognizes that an individual has met predetermined qualifications, usually by passing an examination.\n\n**HIM Certifications (AHIMA):**\n• **RHIT** (Registered Health Information Technician) — requires associate degree from CAHIIM-accredited program; entry-level HIM credential\n• **RHIA** (Registered Health Information Administrator) — requires bachelor's degree from CAHIIM-accredited program; management-level credential\n• **CCS** (Certified Coding Specialist) — inpatient/outpatient coding expertise; mastery of ICD-10-CM/PCS and CPT\n• **CCS-P** (Certified Coding Specialist — Physician-based) — physician office/outpatient coding focus\n• **CDIP** (Certified Documentation Improvement Practitioner) — clinical documentation integrity\n• **CHDA** (Certified Health Data Analyst) — data analytics, reporting, statistical analysis\n\n**AAPC Certifications:**\n• **CPC** (Certified Professional Coder) — physician/outpatient coding (CPT, HCPCS, ICD-10-CM)\n• **COC** (Certified Outpatient Coder), **CIC** (Certified Inpatient Coder)\n\n**Physician Board Certification (ABMS):**\nThe **American Board of Medical Specialties (ABMS)** oversees 24 specialty boards (e.g., American Board of Internal Medicine, American Board of Surgery). Board certification is voluntary but expected — most hospitals require it for medical staff privileges. Physicians must complete **residency training** and pass rigorous specialty exams. Certification now requires **Maintenance of Certification (MOC)** — ongoing assessment every 10 years.",
