            content: "The Privacy Rule defines when PHI can be used or disclosed WITHOUT patient authorization:\n\n**TPO — Treatment, Payment, Operations (No authorization needed):**\n• **Treatment:** Sharing PHI between providers for the patient's care (e.g., referring physician sends records to specialist)\n• **Payment:** Using PHI for billing, claims submission, eligibility verification, prior authorization\n• **Healthcare Operations:** Quality improvement, training, auditing, business planning, credentialing, compliance activities\n\n**Minimum Necessary Standard:**\nFor uses and disclosures OTHER than treatment, covered entities must make **reasonable efforts** to limit PHI to the **minimum necessary** to accomplish the purpose. Does NOT apply to:\n• Disclosures to/requests by the treating provider\n• Disclosures to the individual (patient)\n• Uses/disclosures pursuant to patient authorization\n• Disclosures required by law\n• Disclosures to HHS for HIPAA enforcement\n\n**Other Permitted Disclosures (no authorization required):**\n• **Required by law** (court orders, mandatory reporting)\n• **Public health** activities (disease surveillance, vital statistics)\n• **Abuse/neglect** reporting\n• **Health oversight** activities (licensure surveys, audits)\n• **Judicial proceedings** (with appropriate court order or subpoena with safeguards)\n• **Law enforcement** (limited circumstances — fugitives, crime victims, suspects)\n• **Decedent** information to coroners, medical examiners, funeral directors\n• **Organ/tissue donation** purposes\n• **Research** with IRB/Privacy Board waiver of authorization\n• **Serious threat** to health or safety\n• **Workers' compensation** as authorized by state law\n\n**Authorization Required for:**\nPsychotherapy notes, marketing, sale of PHI, and most other uses not listed above.",
