            content: "**Medicaid Expansion**\nThe ACA originally required ALL states to expand Medicaid eligibility to adults with incomes up to **138% of the Federal Poverty Level (FPL)**. Previously, many states only covered parents, children, pregnant women, elderly, and disabled individuals.\n\n• **NFIB v. Sebelius (2012):** The Supreme Court ruled the mandatory expansion was unconstitutionally coercive — states could not be forced to expand or lose all existing Medicaid funding\n• Result: Expansion became **OPTIONAL** for states\n• As of 2025, **40 states + DC** have expanded; 10 states have not\n• Federal government pays **90%** of expansion costs (down from 100% initially)\n• ~20 million people gained coverage through expansion\n• **Coverage gap:** In non-expansion states, some adults earn too much for Medicaid but too little for marketplace subsidies (below 100% FPL)\n\n**Premium Tax Credits (APTC)**\n• Available to individuals with income **100-400% FPL** (expanded through 2025 by IRA)\n• **Advance Premium Tax Credits (APTC):** Paid directly to insurers monthly\n• Based on the cost of the **second-lowest-cost Silver plan (benchmark plan)**\n• Ensures premiums do not exceed a specified % of income (sliding scale)\n\n**Cost-Sharing Reductions (CSRs)**\n• Available to Silver plan enrollees at **100-250% FPL**\n• Reduce deductibles, copays, and out-of-pocket maximums\n• Federal CSR payments to insurers were discontinued in 2017 — leading to **\"silver loading\"** (insurers raised Silver prices, increasing subsidies)",
