            content: "**Scenario: Two Hospitals Treat Heart Failure Patients**\n\n**Hospital A (Fee-for-Service Focus):**\n• Admits patient, provides treatment, discharges\n• No structured follow-up or care coordination\n• Patient readmitted within 30 days (common: ~23% HF readmission rate)\n• Total cost: $15,000 initial + $12,000 readmission = **$27,000**\n• Quality: 30-day readmission = poor outcome\n• Value = Low quality / High cost = **LOW VALUE**\n\n**Hospital B (Value-Based Focus):**\n• Admits patient, uses multidisciplinary care team\n• CDI specialist ensures accurate HF documentation (HCC coding)\n• Discharge planning: medication reconciliation, home health referral, 48-hour post-discharge call\n• Patient does NOT get readmitted\n• Total cost: $15,000 initial + $500 care coordination = **$15,500**\n• Quality: No readmission = better outcome\n• Value = High quality / Lower cost = **HIGH VALUE**\n\n**Financial Impact Under VBC Programs:**\n• Hospital A: Gets penalized under HRRP (up to 3% Medicare reduction), loses HVBP points, MIPS penalty for attributed physicians\n• Hospital B: Earns MSSP shared savings, avoids HRRP penalty, higher MIPS scores, higher HVBP payment\n\n**HIM Role:** Accurate coding of HF severity (I50.22 vs I50.9), comorbidities for risk adjustment (HCCs), and quality measure documentation directly determines revenue under EVERY value-based program.",
