            content: "**Scenario:** You are the HIM Director at a community hospital. The following three situations arrive on your desk on the same day:\n\n**Situation 1: Subpoena Without Court Order**\nAn attorney sends a subpoena duces tecum requesting the complete medical record of a patient involved in a car accident lawsuit.\n• **Action:** Do NOT immediately release records. HIPAA requires you to make \"reasonable efforts\" to notify the patient or obtain a qualified protective order. Verify the subpoena is valid. Consult your facility's legal counsel. Only release the minimum necessary information after proper authorization or protective order is obtained.\n\n**Situation 2: Coding Compliance Concern**\nA coder reports that a physician has been consistently documenting \"comprehensive\" exams when the documentation only supports \"detailed\" level visits, resulting in higher E/M codes.\n• **Action:** This is a potential **False Claims Act** issue (upcoding). Report to your compliance officer immediately. The FCA's \"reckless disregard\" standard means knowingly submitting these claims = liability. Conduct a chart audit. If confirmed, the facility may need to self-disclose to OIG under the Self-Disclosure Protocol and refund overpayments within 60 days (ACA 60-day rule).\n\n**Situation 3: Physician Referral Question**\nA physician who owns a majority stake in an MRI imaging center asks if she can refer her Medicare patients there.\n• **Action:** This is a potential **Stark Law** violation (physician self-referral to a DHS entity with a financial relationship). Unless a specific exception applies (and you should verify with compliance counsel), this referral is prohibited. Stark is strict liability — no intent required. Violations result in payment denial, refund obligations, and potential FCA liability.",
