      type: "example",
      title: "Medicare Secondary Payer (MSP) Rules",
      content: `# Medicare Secondary Payer (MSP)

By law, Medicare is not always the primary payer — even for Medicare beneficiaries. MSP rules define when Medicare pays **second**.

## When Medicare is SECONDARY

| Situation | Primary Payer | Medicare Role |
|-----------|--------------|--------------|
| Active employer group health plan (20+ employees) | Employer GHP | Secondary |
| Active employer plan (under 20 employees) | Medicare | Primary |
| Workers' Compensation | WC | Secondary |
| Liability insurance (auto, premises) | Liability | Secondary |
| No-fault insurance | No-fault | Secondary |
| VA benefits (for VA-covered conditions) | VA | Secondary |
| ESRD patients (first 30 months) with employer coverage | Employer GHP | Secondary |

## The MSP Questionnaire

Medicare requires providers to ask patients a series of questions to identify MSP situations:
- Are you currently employed?
- Is your spouse currently employed?
- Was this injury/illness work-related?
- Was this injury due to an accident (auto, premises)?

**If any answer is YES** → Investigate whether Medicare is secondary.

## Billing Consequences of Getting MSP Wrong

- Billing Medicare primary when it should be secondary = **False Claims Act violation**
- Medicare may pay erroneously but will seek **recovery** from providers
- Fines up to **$10,957 per false claim**

## ESRD Special Rule

Patients with End-Stage Renal Disease (kidney failure) who have employer coverage:
- Employer plan is **primary for the first 30 months** of ESRD Medicare eligibility
- After 30 months, Medicare becomes **primary**`,
