      type: "concept",
      title: "The 5 Essential CDM Elements",
      content: `## Every CDM Line Has 5 Required Fields

A single row in the Chargemaster must contain all five elements for the claim to be complete and accurate:

### 1. Charge Code (Internal)
- Facility-assigned unique identifier (e.g., 8850001)
- Not visible on the claim; used internally to trigger billing

### 2. Description
- Plain-language name of the service or supply
- Must be clear enough for clinical and billing staff to identify the correct item
- Example: *"CBC with Differential, Automated"*

### 3. Revenue Code
- **4-digit code** (often written with leading zero) assigned by NUBC
- Tells the payer **what category** of service was provided
- Required on UB-04 institutional claims
- Example: **0305** = Lab – Hematology

### 4. CPT / HCPCS Code
- Identifies the **specific procedure or supply** for payer adjudication
- CPT: physician/outpatient procedures (5 digits)
- HCPCS Level II: supplies, drugs, DME (letter + 4 digits)
- Example: **85025** = CBC with automated differential

### 5. Price (Gross Charge)
- The facility's listed charge **before** any payer discounts
- Drives chargemaster-based pricing strategies

> All five elements must be present and accurate. A missing CPT code = claim rejection. A wrong revenue code = payment delay or denial.`,
