      title: "CDM Audit Process",
      content: `## Conducting a CDM Audit

A **CDM audit** systematically reviews chargemaster entries to identify errors, outdated codes, missing items, and compliance risks.

### Audit Scenario: Radiology Department Review

**Setup:** The Revenue Cycle Director asks the CDM Coordinator to audit the Radiology section (320x revenue codes) after a payer identified multiple claim rejections.

---

### Audit Steps

**Step 1 — Extract CDM Data**
Pull all Radiology CDM entries (rev codes 0320–0329). Export to spreadsheet with all 5 CDM elements.

**Step 2 — Compare CPT Codes to Current Year**
Cross-reference every CPT code against the current AMA CPT codebook. Find: CPT **71010** (chest X-ray, 1 view, frontal only) — **deleted** in 2023. Replacement: **71045**.

**Step 3 — Check Revenue Code Accuracy**
Verify every service is mapped to the correct revenue code category. Find: An ultrasound-guided needle biopsy is mapped to 0320 (diagnostic radiology) but should be 0360 (radiology — other) per NUBC guidelines.

**Step 4 — Validate Pricing**
Compare CDM prices to Medicare fee schedule and similar facilities. Find: Chest X-ray priced at $62 — significantly below market; other facilities charge $185–$220.

**Step 5 — Check for Missing Items**
New 3D mammography service added 8 months ago — no CDM entry created. 8 months of services unbilled = **charge leakage**.

**Step 6 — Document Findings & Remediate**
| Finding | Action | Owner |
|---|---|---|
| Deleted CPT 71010 | Update to 71045 | CDM Coordinator |
| Wrong rev code on biopsy | Change to 0369 | CDM Coordinator + Compliance |
| Underpriced chest X-ray | Price review with Finance | Finance Director |
| Missing 3D mammography | Add new CDM line | CDM Coordinator + Radiology |

> Audit findings should be tracked in a formal log with completion dates and verification that claims were corrected.`,
