      type: "concept",
      title: "IPPS Overview & Purpose",
      content: `## Why IPPS Was Created

Before 1983, Medicare paid hospitals based on **actual costs** — the more services provided, the more Medicare paid. This created runaway spending with no incentive for efficiency.

**IPPS changed the incentive structure:**

| Before IPPS | After IPPS |
|---|---|
| Cost-based reimbursement | Fixed prospective payment |
| More services = more revenue | Efficient care = retained margin |
| No efficiency incentive | Hospital bears cost overrun risk |

## Who IPPS Covers
- Medicare Part A fee-for-service beneficiaries
- Acute-care inpatient hospital stays
- Short-term general hospitals (excludes psychiatric, rehab, children's, long-term care)

## Key Principle
**Prospective** means the payment rate is set *before* the stay occurs, based on the patient's diagnosis group — not the actual resources used. If a hospital spends less than the DRG payment, it keeps the difference. If it spends more, it absorbs the loss.

> This is why accurate and complete coding directly affects hospital revenue.`,
