      type: "application",
      title: "Applying OPPS Concepts: Coding Compliance and Revenue Integrity",
      content: `## Real-World Application for HIM Professionals

Understanding APC mechanics is not just academic — it directly impacts **revenue integrity**, **compliance**, and **claim accuracy**.

### Revenue Integrity Responsibilities

**1. Verify Status Indicator Assignments**
Before submitting claims, confirm that the facility's chargemaster correctly reflects the current year's status indicator for each HCPCS code. CMS updates these annually — a code that was payable last year may be packaged this year.

**2. Avoid Unbundling**
Reporting packaged (N) services as if they generate separate payment is a **billing compliance risk**. HCPCS codes with SI=N must be reported on the claim for data purposes but should not be expected to drive additional reimbursement.

**3. Composite APC Awareness**
When billing combinations that trigger composite APCs, verify the claim groups correctly. Partial billing of a composite-eligible service set can result in underpayment or compliance issues.

**4. Pass-Through Tracking**
Monitor CMS notifications for new pass-through approvals and expirations. Billing a drug or device under an expired pass-through status indicator results in underpayment.

### Documentation Requirements

| Requirement | Why It Matters |
|-------------|----------------|
| Complete procedure documentation | Supports medical necessity for each APC |
| Physician attestation for hospital services | Required for facility-only billing |
| Itemized charges on UB-04 | All services must appear — even packaged N codes |
| Modifier usage | Modifiers signal special circumstances (bilateral, reduced, discontinued) |

### Key Compliance Risk: Chargemaster Accuracy

The chargemaster (CDM) must be updated **every January 1** when the new OPPS Final Rule takes effect. Failure to update means:
- Services coded with last year's SI may be billed incorrectly
- New composite APC triggers may be missed
- Pass-through expirations may go unrecognized

> **Best practice:** Schedule an annual CDM review each December to prepare for January 1 OPPS changes.`,
