      type: "quiz",
      title: "Quiz 1: Status Indicators",
      content: `## Knowledge Check: Status Indicators

### Question 1
A hospital outpatient coder is reviewing a claim for a patient who had a colonoscopy (CPT 45378, SI=T). During the same visit, the patient also received IV ondansetron for nausea (HCPCS J2405, SI=N).

**Which of the following statements is CORRECT?**

A) Both the colonoscopy and the ondansetron will generate separate APC payments

B) The ondansetron will generate a separate drug APC; the colonoscopy will not be paid because it is surgical

C) The ondansetron is packaged — no separate APC payment is generated for it, but it must still be coded on the claim

D) Neither service generates an APC payment because the patient had both a surgical procedure and a drug on the same day

---

### Answer: C

**Explanation:**

Status Indicator **T** (Surgical Procedure) means the colonoscopy (45378) IS paid separately under OPPS — it generates a surgical APC.

Status Indicator **N** (Packaged Service) means the ondansetron (J2405) does NOT generate a separate APC payment. Its cost is considered bundled into the primary APC (the colonoscopy). However, the coder **must still report** J2405 on the UB-04 claim form for data integrity and CMS cost reporting purposes.

**Why the other options are wrong:**
- **A** is wrong: N-coded services don't generate separate payment
- **B** is wrong: T-coded surgical procedures ARE paid; N drugs are not paid separately
- **D** is wrong: T procedures are always paid; N services are bundled, not negating the surgical payment

> **Key rule:** SI=N → code it, but don't expect payment. SI=T or S → paid separately (with possible multiple-procedure discount).`,
