      type: "example",
      title: "LTACH vs. IRF vs. SNF: Choosing the Right PAC Setting",
      content: `## Case Comparison: Three Patients, Three Settings

### Understanding the Placement Decision

Correct PAC placement is a **clinical, regulatory, and financial** decision. Each setting has criteria, and incorrect placement creates compliance risk.

---

### Patient A — Mr. Thompson, 65M, Ventilator-Dependent
**Condition**: COPD exacerbation, ventilator 8 days, tracheostomy, multiple comorbidities

| Criterion | Assessment |
|-----------|-----------|
| Medically stable? | Marginal — still ventilator-dependent |
| Therapy needs? | Minimal (bedbound) |
| Expected LOS? | 30–45 days |
| **→ Best Setting** | **LTACH** (MS-LTC-DRG, qualifies for non-site-neutral rate) |

---

### Patient B — Mrs. Chen, 72F, Post-Stroke
**Condition**: Left hemiplegia, aphasia, dysphagia, cognitively intact, motivated

| Criterion | Assessment |
|-----------|-----------|
| Can tolerate 3 hrs therapy/day? | Yes |
| Qualifying IRF condition? | Yes (stroke) |
| Functional improvement expected? | High potential |
| **→ Best Setting** | **IRF** (CMG-based, stroke qualifies under 60% rule) |

---

### Patient C — Mr. Patel, 80M, Hip Replacement
**Condition**: Elective THA, ambulatory with walker, mild cognitive impairment, family support

| Criterion | Assessment |
|-----------|-----------|
| Medically complex? | No |
| Needs 3 hrs/day? | Unlikely (mild impairment) |
| Home with HH possible? | Yes, but family prefers SNF short-term |
| **→ Best Setting** | **SNF** (PDPM, PT/OT components; later home health) |

---

**Key Takeaway**: Placement in wrong setting = compliance violations, billing fraud risk, and suboptimal patient outcomes.`,
