      type: "concept",
      title: "Geographic Practice Cost Index (GPCI)",
      content: `## Adjusting for Local Cost Differences

The same physician service costs more to deliver in Manhattan than in rural Mississippi. The **Geographic Practice Cost Index (GPCI)** corrects for this by adjusting each RVU component for local costs.

### One GPCI per RVU Component
| GPCI | Adjusts For | What It Reflects |
|------|------------|-----------------|
| **wGPCI** | Work RVU | Physician earnings relative to national average |
| **peGPCI** | Practice Expense RVU | Local cost of staff, rent, supplies |
| **mpGPCI** | Malpractice RVU | Local malpractice insurance market rates |

### How GPCIs Work
- National average = **1.000**
- High-cost area (e.g., San Francisco): GPCI > 1.000
- Low-cost area (e.g., rural Alabama): GPCI < 1.000

### Legislative Floors
Congress has periodically set **floors** on the work GPCI (e.g., 1.000 minimum) to protect rural and underserved areas from reduced payments.

### Payment Localities
CMS divides the U.S. into **payment localities** — geographic areas sharing the same GPCI values. Some states have a single statewide locality; others (like California) have multiple.

> **Key insight:** GPCI adjustments ensure that Medicare pays appropriately for the *actual cost environment* of the provider's location.`,
