      type: "concept",
      title: "Interpreting & Benchmarking CMI",
      content: `## What Does Your CMI Mean?

### The Baseline: 1.00
CMS sets the national average DRG relative weight at **1.00**. Your CMI tells you how your patient population compares:

| CMI Range | Interpretation |
|-----------|----------------|
| < 0.90 | Significantly below average complexity |
| 0.90 – 1.09 | Near average (community hospitals) |
| 1.10 – 1.29 | Above average (regional hospitals) |
| 1.30 – 1.59 | High complexity (teaching hospitals) |
| ≥ 1.60 | Very high complexity (major academic centers) |

### Benchmarking Considerations
CMI comparisons are only valid when comparing **like to like**:
- **Bed size**: 100-bed community vs. 500-bed academic is not apples-to-apples
- **Teaching status**: Residency programs attract complex cases
- **Payer mix**: Medicare-heavy populations skew CMI differently than Medicaid-heavy
- **Geographic market**: Trauma centers and specialty services elevate CMI
- **All-payer vs. Medicare-only CMI**: Medicare CMI is the most commonly benchmarked

### Red Flags in CMI Benchmarking
- CMI **declining** quarter-over-quarter → documentation or coding gaps emerging
- CMI **below peer group** despite similar acuity → CDI opportunity
- CMI **suddenly spiking** → audit for overcoding risk
- **Service line CMI** outliers → investigate documentation practices in those units`,
