      type: "concept",
      title: "CPT Code Structure: Three Categories",
      content: `## CPT Code Categories

The AMA publishes CPT codes annually. All codes are five characters, but their meaning varies by category.

### Category I — Main Codebook (00100–99499)
Organized into **six sections**:

| Range | Section |
|-------|---------|
| 00100–01999 | Anesthesia |
| 10004–69990 | Surgery |
| 70010–79999 | Radiology |
| 80047–89398 | Pathology & Laboratory |
| 90281–99199 | Medicine |
| 99202–99499 | E/M |

### Category II — Performance Measurement (4 digits + F)
- Supplemental tracking codes (e.g., **2000F** blood pressure recorded)
- **Optional** — never replace Category I codes
- Used for quality reporting programs (MIPS)

### Category III — Emerging Technology (4 digits + T)
- Temporary codes for new procedures lacking Category I status
- Payers may or may not reimburse
- **Must be reported instead of an unlisted code** when one exists
- Reviewed every 5 years; codes either advance to Category I or are deleted

> **Key rule:** When a Category III code exists for a service, use it — do not use an unlisted procedure code.`,
