      type: "example",
      title: "Correct Coding vs. Upcoding — The Critical Distinction",
      content: `## Correct Coding vs. Upcoding

Understanding this distinction is essential for compliance. The line between optimization and fraud is **documentation**.

### Correct Coding (Compliant)
> Billing the most accurate, specific code that reflects the documented service.

- Assigning the highest-severity E&M level when documentation **supports** it
- Capturing all procedure components that were actually performed and documented
- Using the most specific ICD-10 code for the confirmed diagnosis
- Querying the physician to clarify documentation → coding to the clarified record

### Upcoding (Fraudulent)
> Billing a higher-level or more complex code than what was actually documented or performed.

- Billing Level 5 E&M when documentation only supports Level 3
- Adding a diagnosis code for a condition that was not addressed or confirmed
- Using a more complex procedure code than what was performed
- Coaching physicians to document higher acuity without clinical basis

### Key Differentiator: Documentation
| Situation | Action | Result |
|-----------|--------|--------|
| Documentation clearly supports high-level code | Bill accordingly | Correct coding ✓ |
| Documentation is unclear — query physician | Clarify, then code | Correct coding ✓ |
| Documentation is weak — bill higher code anyway | Upcoding | Fraud ✗ |
| Instruct physician to "add" findings not present | Fabrication | Fraud ✗ |

### Modifier Use: Appropriate vs. Inappropriate
| Modifier | Appropriate Use | Inappropriate Use |
|----------|----------------|------------------|
| -25 | Separate, significant E&M same day as procedure — fully documented | Routine evaluation bundled with procedure to inflate payment |
| -59 | Distinct procedural service, different anatomy/session | Used to bypass NCCI edit without clinical basis |
| -22 | Increased complexity — documented by operative report | Added to increase reimbursement without documented basis |`,
