      content: `## Quiz: Unbundling Identification

**Question 2**

A hospital laboratory consistently bills **individual CPT codes for each component** of a Basic Metabolic Panel (BMP) when all components are ordered together:
- 82565 (Creatinine), 84132 (Potassium), 82947 (Glucose), 84295 (Sodium), 82374 (CO₂), 82435 (Chloride), 82310 (Calcium), 84520 (BUN)

The correct BMP panel code is **CPT 80048**.

**Which statement BEST describes this billing practice?**

---

**A)** This is correct coding — each test is clinically distinct and can be billed individually regardless of how they were ordered.

**B)** This is unbundling — billing components separately when a comprehensive panel code exists inflates reimbursement and violates NCCI rules.

**C)** This is permissible as long as the individual codes are billed on separate claim lines.

**D)** This is acceptable only if the payer does not have a contract that references the panel code.

---

**Select the best answer before reading below.**

---

### Answer: **B**

**Explanation:**

When a panel (BMP = CPT 80048) is ordered, all components are considered **bundled** into the panel code. Billing each component individually when they were ordered as a panel is the textbook definition of **unbundling**.

- **NCCI has panel edits** — individual component codes are Column 2 codes to the panel code
- Billing individually generates significantly more reimbursement than the panel rate
- This is a **False Claims Act violation** if done intentionally

**Why the other answers are wrong:**
- **(A)** Clinical distinctness does not override NCCI panel bundling rules when ordered together
- **(C)** Separate claim lines do not bypass NCCI edits — they are applied per date of service
- **(D)** NCCI is a CMS rule, not a payer-contract term — it applies regardless of contract language

**When individual billing IS appropriate:**
- Only specific components were ordered (not the full panel)
- A stand-alone test was performed on a different date
- Medical necessity supports individual test ordering rather than the panel

> **Revenue integrity takeaway:** Charge capture and coding teams must recognize panel orders and apply the panel code, not split the components for revenue gain.`,
      answer: "B",
      explanation: "Billing individual component codes when the complete panel was ordered is unbundling — NCCI panel edits bundle the components into the panel code (CPT 80048). Separate claim lines do not override NCCI edits. Intentional unbundling is a False Claims Act violation.",
