      content: `# Level 2: QIC Reconsideration

If Level 1 is unfavorable, the appeal moves to the **Qualified Independent Contractor (QIC)** — a contractor that is independent from the MAC that issued the denial.

## Key Facts
- **Who reviews:** Qualified Independent Contractor (QIC) — NOT the MAC
- **Filing deadline:** **180 days** from the date of the MAC redetermination notice
- **Decision timeframe:** QIC must respond within **60 days**
- **No dollar threshold** — any denied amount qualifies
- **Independence is key:** QIC has no financial relationship with the MAC

## Why Level 2 Matters
- The QIC provides a **truly independent review**
- QIC reviewers include physicians and clinical professionals
- Overturn rates can be **higher than Level 1** because the review is genuinely independent
- This is the **last level** before requiring a minimum dollar amount in controversy

## What to Submit
All documentation from Level 1, PLUS:
- Any new medical records or clinical evidence
- A written statement of why the MAC's decision was wrong
- Expert opinion letters (if applicable)
- Relevant Medicare coverage policies (LCDs, NCDs)

## Outcome Options
| Result | Next Step |
|--------|-----------|
| Fully favorable | Claim paid, done |
| Partially favorable | Appeal remainder |
| Unfavorable | File for ALJ Hearing (Level 3) within 60 days |

> **Note:** At Level 2, you can **submit additional evidence** up until the QIC issues its decision. After that, the record is generally closed for higher levels.`,
