      content: `# Building a Strong Appeal: Documentation Package

## Case Scenario
A skilled nursing facility receives a Medicare Part A denial for a patient's 14-day stay, stating services were "not medically necessary." The MAC denied the $9,200 claim. The facility is preparing a Level 2 QIC appeal.

## The Documentation Package

### 1. Cover Letter / Statement of Appeal
- Clear statement of what is being appealed and why the denial was incorrect
- Reference to relevant Local Coverage Determination (LCD) or National Coverage Determination (NCD)
- Summary of key clinical facts supporting medical necessity

### 2. Medical Records (Chronological)
- Physician's certification of need
- Daily nursing notes showing skilled care provided
- Therapy evaluation and progress notes (PT, OT, SLP)
- Physician orders throughout the stay
- Discharge summary

### 3. Medicare Coverage Policy Citations
- Relevant LCD (e.g., "Skilled Nursing Facility Services — LCD L12345")
- CMS Benefit Policy Manual citations (Chapter 8 for SNF)
- Any applicable CMS transmittals or clarifications

### 4. Remittance Advice
- Original Medicare RA showing the denial reason code
- All prior denial notices

### 5. Additional Evidence (if available)
- Physician attestation letter explaining medical necessity
- Functional assessment scores (FIM, MDS data)
- Comparison to similar approved cases (if documented)

## Documentation Best Practices
| Do | Don't |
|----|-------|
| Organize chronologically | Submit disorganized records |
| Highlight key passages | Make reviewer search for evidence |
| Cite specific policy criteria | Make vague clinical arguments |
| Include ALL relevant records | Omit records that seem unfavorable |
| Submit new evidence at Level 2 | Wait until Level 3 (record closes) |`,
