      type: "example",
      title: "Commercial Payer Appeals & IRO Process",
      content: `# Commercial Payer Appeals & Independent Review Organizations (IROs)

## Commercial Payer Appeal Processes

Unlike Medicare's standardized five-level federal process, **commercial payer appeals vary significantly by plan**. However, most follow a similar structure:

### Typical Commercial Appeal Levels
| Level | Reviewer | Common Timeframe |
|-------|----------|-----------------|
| Internal Level 1 | Payer's medical reviewers | 30–60 days |
| Internal Level 2 | Payer's Medical Director | 30–60 days |
| External IRO | Independent Review Organization | 30–45 days |
| State Regulators | State insurance commissioner | Varies |
| Civil Court | Judicial review (ERISA plans) | Open |

### Key Differences from Medicare
- **Deadlines vary**: Typically 30–180 days (read your contract and plan EOB)
- **No standardized forms**: Each payer has its own process
- **Contracted rates matter**: The denied amount may be different from charges
- **ERISA preemption**: Self-funded employer plans follow federal ERISA law, not state insurance law

---

## Independent Review Organization (IRO)

An **IRO** is an independent, accredited organization that reviews coverage disputes between patients/providers and insurance companies.

### When IRO Review Applies
- After exhausting internal payer appeals (usually 2 internal levels)
- For **urgent/expedited** cases: decision within 72 hours
- For **standard** cases: decision within 30–45 days
- Required by state law for most fully-insured commercial plans

### IRO vs. ALJ: Key Comparison
| Feature | IRO (Commercial) | ALJ (Medicare) |
|---------|-----------------|---------------|
| Triggered after | Internal appeals | Level 2 QIC |
| Timeline | 30–45 days standard | 90 days (backlogs) |
| Binding? | Yes (on insurer) | Yes |
| Dollar threshold | Usually none | ~$180 |
| Cost | Usually free to provider | Free |

> **Important:** For **self-funded ERISA** plans, IRO review may not be available at the state level — appeal goes directly to federal ERISA claim procedures and potentially federal court.`,
