      type: "concept",
      title: "Authorization, Precertification & Timely Filing",
      content: `## Managing Payer Requirements

Failure to comply with authorization and timely filing rules is one of the most preventable causes of claim denials.

### Prior Authorization / Precertification
- **Precertification**: Payer approval required BEFORE rendering non-emergency services
- Applies to: surgeries, inpatient admissions, specialty referrals, high-cost imaging (MRI, PET)
- Failure to obtain auth = claim denial (often non-appealable)
- Document auth number on the claim (Box 23 on CMS-1500)

### Concurrent Review
- Ongoing authorization during an inpatient stay
- Utilization management (UM) nurse reviews medical necessity periodically
- Days not authorized are not reimbursed

### Retrospective Review
- Review of services already rendered (often for emergencies)
- Provider must submit clinical documentation within defined timeframe

### Timely Filing Deadlines
- Defined in the contract — typically **90–365 days** from date of service
- Failure to file within the deadline = permanent denial (no appeal right in most contracts)
- Track filing deadlines by payer in your practice management system

### Best Practices
| Requirement | Action |
|---|---|
| Auth required | Verify before service; document auth # |
| Concurrent review | Respond to UM requests within 24–48 hrs |
| Timely filing | Submit within 30 days of service to buffer |
| Denial for no auth | Appeal with clinical documentation immediately |`,
