      type: "concept",
      title: "Internal vs. External Audits",
      content: `## Audit Types: Purposes and Scope

### Internal Audits
Conducted **by the organization's own compliance or HIM staff**.

| Feature | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Purpose** | Identify and correct errors before external review |
| **Frequency** | Ongoing (monthly, quarterly, annually) |
| **Scope** | Targeted by payer, DRG, coder, or service line |
| **Outcome** | Education, corrective action, process improvement |
| **Privilege** | May be attorney-client privileged if structured properly |

**Types of internal audits:**
- **Prospective** – Review before claim submission (catches errors early)
- **Concurrent** – Review during the encounter or admission
- **Retrospective** – Review after claim submission (most common)

### External Audits
Conducted **by outside entities** — payers, government agencies, or consultants.

| Auditor | Full Name | Focus |
|---|---|---|
| **RAC** | Recovery Audit Contractor | Medicare overpayments |
| **MAC** | Medicare Administrative Contractor | Local coverage, billing |
| **OIG** | Office of Inspector General | Fraud, waste, abuse |
| **ZPIC/UPICs** | Zone Program Integrity Contractors | Fraud investigation |
| **Commercial payers** | Insurance companies | Contract compliance |

> **Key difference**: Internal audits are self-corrective. External audits are investigative and can result in **repayment demands, exclusions, or prosecution**.`,
