      title: "OIG Work Plan: High-Risk Areas",
      content: `## Office of Inspector General (OIG) Work Plan

The OIG publishes an annual Work Plan identifying **high-risk areas** for Medicare and Medicaid fraud, waste, and abuse. Coding departments must monitor these areas closely.

### Current High-Risk Coding Areas

#### 1. Sepsis DRGs (MS-DRG 870–872)
- Sepsis is frequently **overcoded** without meeting clinical criteria
- Must distinguish: SIRS vs. sepsis vs. severe sepsis vs. septic shock
- **Sepsis-3 criteria** required: documented infection + organ dysfunction
- High-weight DRGs attract RAC attention

#### 2. Short-Stay / 1-Day Inpatient Stays
- Admissions lasting <2 midnights are presumed **outpatient** (2-Midnight Rule)
- Inpatient admission of a 1-day stay may be denied as **medically unnecessary**
- Physician must document medical necessity for inpatient level of care

#### 3. E/M Upcoding
- Higher-level E/M codes (99205, 99215, 99285) warrant more scrutiny
- Must be supported by **documentation of medical decision-making or time**
- Systematic upcoding patterns trigger audits

#### 4. Other OIG Targets
| Area | Risk |
|---|---|
| Modifier 25 (separate E/M) | Must be distinct from procedure |
| Modifier 59 / XU unbundling | Must reflect truly separate services |
| Physician self-referral billing | Stark Law compliance |
| Home health claims | Face-to-face encounter requirements |

> **Tip**: Review the OIG Work Plan at **oig.hhs.gov** each October when the new plan is released.`,
