      content: `## The 7 Elements — OIG Compliance Guidance

The OIG published guidance outlining **7 elements** every effective compliance program must contain. These apply to hospitals, physician practices, and all healthcare entities billing federal programs.

### The 7 Elements

| # | Element | Core Requirement |
|---|---|---|
| **1** | Written Policies & Procedures | Standards of conduct; coding and billing policies |
| **2** | Compliance Officer & Committee | Designated leader accountable for the program |
| **3** | Education & Training | All employees trained on compliance expectations |
| **4** | Effective Communication | Open-door policy; hotlines; no retaliation |
| **5** | Internal Monitoring & Auditing | Ongoing audits to detect problems proactively |
| **6** | Discipline & Enforcement | Consistent consequences for violations |
| **7** | Response & Prevention | Investigate, correct, and prevent recurrence |

### Real-World Application: Coding Department

**Element 1** → Coding policy manual with ICD-10-CM/PCS guidelines, query policies
**Element 2** → Compliance Officer (often also HIM Director in smaller facilities)
**Element 3** → Annual coding compliance training; new coder orientation
**Element 4** → Anonymous hotline for coders to report pressure to upcode
**Element 5** → Quarterly internal coding audits by DRG and coder
**Element 6** → Progressive discipline for repeated coding errors; coder certification requirements
**Element 7** → Root cause analysis after audit findings; policy updates

> **Key point**: The OIG gives credit to organizations that have genuine compliance programs — they receive reduced penalties in settlement negotiations. A program "on paper only" provides no protection.`,
