      content: `## What Is the OIG Work Plan?\n\nThe **Office of Inspector General (OIG)** publishes an annual **Work Plan** listing areas it will audit, investigate, or evaluate during the fiscal year. Updated monthly at **oig.hhs.gov**.\n\n### Why HIM Must Monitor It\n- Signals where **federal auditors will focus**\n- Work Plan items become **RAC (Recovery Audit Contractor) targets**\n- Hospitals in audits face **extrapolation** — sample error rate multiplied across all similar claims\n- Proactive internal auditing prevents large repayment demands\n\n### Common Coding-Related Work Plan Items\n| Area | Focus |\n|---|---|\n| Sepsis DRG 870–872 | Severity specificity — shock vs septicemia |\n| Heart Failure DRG 291–293 | Systolic/diastolic/combined, acute vs chronic |\n| Short inpatient stays | Two-midnight rule medical necessity |\n| Post-acute transfer DRGs | Discharge status accuracy |\n| Inpatient vs outpatient status | Observation vs admission criteria |\n\n### HIM Response Protocol\n1. **Monitor** Work Plan monthly\n2. **Pull a sample** (25–30 cases) in flagged DRG categories\n3. **Audit** coding accuracy against documentation\n4. **Remediate** — education and process correction\n5. **Document** the audit trail — demonstrates good faith\n\n> If OIG is auditing it, your hospital should already be auditing it internally.`,
