      content: `## The 7 Elements of an Effective Compliance Program\n\nOIG guidance establishes 7 structural elements. For HIM, these form the framework for preventing and detecting fraud.\n\n| # | Element | HIM Application |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Written Policies & Procedures** | Coding guidelines, query policies, escalation SOPs |\n| 2 | **Compliance Officer & Committee** | HIM Director + CCO, CDI committee |\n| 3 | **Education & Training** | Annual compliance training, new-hire orientation |\n| 4 | **Effective Communication** | Anonymous hotline, no-retaliation policy |\n| 5 | **Auditing & Monitoring** | PEPPER, RAC tracking, focused audits |\n| 6 | **Enforcement & Discipline** | Consistent consequences, documented responses |\n| 7 | **Responding to Problems** | Voluntary self-disclosure, repayment, root cause analysis |\n\n### Element 5 — HIM's Primary Contribution\n- **Prospective**: Before claim submission\n- **Concurrent**: During episode of care\n- **Retrospective**: After submission — PEPPER-driven\n- **Focused**: High-risk DRGs from OIG Work Plan\n\n### Element 7 — Voluntary Self-Disclosure Protocol (SDP)\n1. Stop the practice immediately\n2. Quantify the overpayment\n3. Disclose to OIG via SDP\n4. Repay with interest\n- **Result**: Minimum 1.5× damages vs up to 3× if government finds it first\n\n> A compliance program that exists only on paper provides no protection. OIG evaluates **operational effectiveness**.`,
