      title: "DRG Creep — Systematic Pattern vs. Legitimate CMI Improvement",
      content: `## What Is DRG Creep?\n\nA systematic pattern of coding shifts toward higher-weighted DRGs not supported by actual changes in patient acuity or documentation quality.\n\n### DRG Creep vs. Legitimate CMI Improvement\n| Indicator | DRG Creep | Legitimate CDI |\n|---|---|---|\n| Speed | Sudden jump | Gradual over months |\n| Documentation | Cannot explain with records | Every code documented |\n| Physician involvement | Unaware or pressured | Active CDI participation |\n| Query agreement rate | ~100% (leading queries) | 85–90% (expected) |\n| CMI change rate | >0.1 in one quarter | 0.02–0.08/year |\n\n### Statistical Detection Methods\n- **Benchmark**: National average MCC% for each DRG\n- **Flag**: Hospital >2 standard deviations above expected → automated review\n- **Trending**: Sudden CMI change without documented CDI initiative\n\n### Industry CDI Benchmarks\nWell-executed CDI program achieves **0.02–0.08 CMI points per year** (years 1–3). Larger sudden improvements trigger OIG scrutiny.\n\n### Root Causes of DRG Creep\n1. **Management pressure**: "CMI needs to improve this quarter"\n2. **Leading queries**: "Patient had septic shock, correct?" ← NOT compliant\n3. **Code selection bias**: Always choosing higher-paying code when ambiguous\n4. **Coder CMI scorecards**: Evaluating coders on CMI contribution (compliance risk)\n\n> Evaluating coders individually on CMI contribution creates a fraud incentive. Both AHIMA and OIG flag this practice.`,
