      title: "Compliance Audit Scenario — Coder Receives Pressure to Change Principal Diagnosis",
      content: `## Scenario: DRG Improvement Request\n\n**Setting**: Inpatient hospital coding department, post-discharge review\n\n**Clinical Summary**: 72-year-old admitted with acute exacerbation of COPD. Treated with bronchodilators, steroids. Discharged in 4 days. No documentation of respiratory failure.\n\n**Code Assigned by Coder**: MS-DRG 192 (COPD — MCC not present)\n\n**Message from Revenue Cycle Manager**: *"Can you review this case again? If we can get respiratory failure documented, it would move this to DRG 189 and add about $4,500. The physician uses resp failure terms loosely in clinic notes."*\n\n---\n\n### Analysis of the Request\n\n| Element | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Clinical documentation | No respiratory failure documented in inpatient record |\n| Query justification | No conflicting or ambiguous documentation — no basis for query |\n| Request basis | Financial (DRG improvement) — not clinical clarification |\n| AHIMA Standard violated | Standard 3: never code for reimbursement |\n| FCA risk | Assigning unsupported code = potentially false claim |\n\n### Correct Coder Response\n1. **Do not** assign respiratory failure code — no documentation in inpatient record\n2. Clinic notes from prior encounters cannot be used to code inpatient admission without current encounter documentation\n3. **Respond in writing**: "There is no documentation of respiratory failure in the inpatient record. I am unable to assign this code without physician documentation in this encounter. I am happy to send a compliant query if you believe the physician intended to document this condition."\n4. **Document** the exchange — email trail\n5. If manager persists: **escalate to compliance officer**\n\n### What a Compliant Query Would Look Like (IF Basis Existed)\n*"During this encounter, the patient required bronchodilators and steroids for respiratory distress. Please clarify: Did the patient have acute respiratory failure? If so, was it hypoxic, hypercapnic, or combined? Or is the clinical picture better described as acute exacerbation of COPD without respiratory failure? Or other: ___"*`,
