      title: "Risk Adjustment & HCC Coding",
      content: `## Why Risk Adjustment Matters

Medicare Advantage (MA) plans receive monthly payments from CMS based on how **sick** their enrolled members are. This system — called **risk adjustment** — ensures plans get paid more for sicker patients and less for healthier ones.

At the heart of this system is the **Hierarchical Condition Category (HCC)** model, which translates ICD-10 diagnoses into a **Risk Adjustment Factor (RAF) score**.

### The Core Equation
> **RAF Score × Capitation Rate = Monthly Payment per Member**

### Why Accurate HCC Coding Matters
- **Undercoding** → Plan receives insufficient funds → Care quality suffers
- **Overcoding** → Fraudulent overpayment → Federal penalties (False Claims Act)
- **Accurate coding** → Appropriate payment → Sustainable care for complex patients

### What You Will Learn
1. How the HCC model works and its purpose
2. RAF score components
3. CMS-HCC V28 vs V24 differences
4. MEAT documentation criteria
5. Annual re-documentation requirements
6. HCC hierarchy rules
7. RADV audit methodology
8. High-value HCC categories`,
