      type: "concept",
      title: "HCC Model Purpose & Structure",
      content: `## Hierarchical Condition Category (HCC) Model

The HCC model was developed by CMS to predict **future healthcare costs** for Medicare Advantage members based on their diagnosed conditions.

### How HCCs Are Organized
- ICD-10-CM diagnosis codes are grouped into **~9,000 condition categories (CCs)**
- Clinically related CCs are organized into **HCC hierarchies**
- Only the **most severe condition** in each hierarchy is counted (hence "Hierarchical")

### HCC Model Versions
| Feature | V24 | V28 (Current) |
|---|---|---|
| HCC Categories | 86 | 115 |
| Transition Period | Through 2024 | Fully effective 2025 |
| Specificity | Lower | Higher (more ICD-10 specific) |
| New Categories | — | Dementia severity, CKD stages |

### Why the Hierarchy Exists
If a patient has **both** Type 2 diabetes with complications AND Type 2 diabetes without complications:
- Both codes map to HCCs
- Only the **more severe** HCC (with complications) is counted
- This prevents double-counting of related conditions

### HCC Assignment Rules
1. Each ICD-10 code maps to zero or one CC
2. CCs within the same hierarchy → only highest severity counted
3. CCs in **different** hierarchies → **both** counted (additive)`,
