      type: "concept",
      title: "Capitation Payments & RAF Scores",
      content: `## How MA Plans Are Paid: PMPM Capitation

### Per-Member-Per-Month (PMPM)
CMS pays MA plans a **fixed monthly amount** for each enrolled member — regardless of how much care that member uses. This shifts financial risk from CMS to the MA plan.

### Risk Adjustment Factor (RAF) Score
The RAF score reflects the **predicted health cost** of an enrollee compared to an average Medicare beneficiary (RAF = 1.0).

**RAF is calculated from:**
- **Demographics:** Age, sex, Medicaid eligibility, institutional status
- **Hierarchical Condition Categories (HCCs):** Chronic diagnosis codes submitted from the prior year

### HCC → RAF → Revenue Pipeline
\`\`\`
Patient diagnosed with diabetes with CKD
        ↓
Provider submits ICD-10: E11.65 (HCC 18 + HCC 138)
        ↓
CMS maps diagnosis to HCC weight
        ↓
Adds to patient's RAF score
        ↓
Higher PMPM payment to MA plan next year
\`\`\`

### Why Accuracy Matters
| Scenario | RAF Impact | Plan Revenue |
|---|---|---|
| All HCCs captured | Full RAF | Adequate to cover care costs |
| HCC missed (gap) | RAF too low | Plan underpaid — loses money |
| HCC unsupported | RAF too high | CMS overpaid → RADV repayment |

> **Bottom line:** MA plans profit from **accurate** risk coding — not from inflating codes.`,
