      type: "example",
      title: "HCC Capture Gap at Annual Wellness Visits",
      content: `## Real-World Scenario: Missed HCCs at the AWV

### Background
The **Annual Wellness Visit (AWV)** — billed as G0438 (first) or G0439 (subsequent) — is a **preventive visit** that MA plans prioritize because it is a prime opportunity to review and document all active chronic conditions.

### The Problem: HCC Capture Gaps
Many providers treat the AWV as a brief wellness check and fail to:
- Review the **problem list** for active chronic conditions
- **Re-document** diagnoses that qualify as HCCs
- Code to **maximum specificity** (e.g., "diabetes" vs. "T2DM with CKD")

### Case Study: Mrs. Patterson, 72, MA Enrollee

**Her active conditions (from prior year):**
- Type 2 diabetes mellitus with CKD stage 3 (HCC 18 + HCC 138)
- Congestive heart failure, systolic, chronic (HCC 85)
- COPD with acute exacerbation (HCC 111)

**What the provider documented at AWV:**
\`\`\`
A/P: Annual wellness visit completed.
     - Continue current medications
     - Follow up with cardiology
     - Labs ordered
Codes submitted: G0439 only
\`\`\`

**Result:**
- **Zero HCCs captured** for this visit
- All 4 HCCs must be documented by at least one provider each calendar year
- If no other encounter documents them → RAF score drops → plan underpaid by ~$2,100/yr for this member alone

### Correct Documentation
\`\`\`
A/P: 1. T2DM with CKD stage 3, stable — continue metformin (E11.65)
     2. CHF systolic, chronic — managed with carvedilol (I50.22)
     3. COPD — currently stable on tiotropium (J44.1)
     4. Annual wellness visit completed (G0439)
\`\`\``,
