      type: "concept",
      title: "ICD-10-CM Annual Update Cycle & Code Freeze",
      content: `# ICD-10-CM Annual Update Cycle

## The Update Cycle
ICD-10-CM codes are updated **once per year**, with all new, revised, and deleted codes taking effect on:

> **October 1** — the effective date for every ICD-10-CM update

This date is set by CMS and the National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS) and applies to all payers and providers.

## The Code Freeze Period

### What Is a Code Freeze?
A **code freeze** is the period during which no new codes may be added or revised, allowing the industry time to prepare for the upcoming October 1 changes.

### Freeze Timeline
| Period | Activity |
|---|---|
| January 1 – ~April | Proposals submitted to NCHS |
| ~April – September 30 | **Code Freeze** — no new codes added |
| October 1 | New fiscal year codes take effect |
| October – December | Coders apply updated code set |

> **Exception**: Emergency codes (e.g., COVID-19 in 2020) may be added outside the freeze period with federal authorization.

## Why This Matters for HIM Coders
1. **Transition readiness** — coders must be trained on new codes before Oct 1
2. **Encoder updates** — coding software must be updated before the effective date
3. **Claim rejection risk** — submitting a deleted or invalid code after Oct 1 results in denial
4. **Audit exposure** — using a code from the wrong year can indicate systemic billing errors

## ICD-10-PCS Updates
ICD-10-PCS (inpatient procedures) follows the **same October 1 cycle** — often with more extensive additions due to new surgical technologies and device categories.

## Practical Workflow
\`\`\`
Late Summer: Obtain updated code book / encoder update
September: Complete coder training on new/revised codes
October 1: Go live with updated code set
October – December: Monitor for coding errors with new codes
\`\`\``,
