      type: "concept",
      title: "The Daily Inpatient Census",
      content: `## What Is the Daily Inpatient Census?

The **daily inpatient census** is the count of inpatients present in a hospital at a single, defined point in time — **midnight (12:00 AM)**.

### Why Midnight?
Midnight was chosen because it is the quietest point of the hospital's operational day, minimizing the chance of counting a patient twice (once as admitted, once as discharged). It creates a clean 24-hour accounting period.

### Who Is Counted?
A patient is included in the midnight census if they occupied a bed **at the stroke of midnight**, regardless of:
- Whether they were awake or asleep
- Whether they were in their room or temporarily in another department (e.g., the ICU, radiology)

> **Key rule:** If the patient's head was on a hospital pillow at midnight, they are in the census.

### The Admission & Discharge Register (A&D Log)
Hospitals maintain an **admission and discharge register** (also called the A&D log) — a chronological record of every patient admitted and every patient discharged throughout the day. This register is the source document used to calculate each day's census.

### Inpatient vs. Outpatient
Census statistics apply **only to inpatients** — patients formally admitted to the hospital. Outpatients (those who receive services without being admitted overnight) are tracked separately and are **never** included in the daily inpatient census.`,
