      type: "concept",
      title: "Percentage of Occupancy Formula",
      content: `## The Core Formula

The **percentage of occupancy** for inpatient beds (adult and pediatric) is:

$$\\text{Percentage of Occupancy} = \\frac{\\text{Total Inpatient Service Days}}{\\text{Available Beds} \\times \\text{Days in Period}} \\times 100$$

### Breaking It Down

| Component | What It Means |
|-----------|---------------|
| **Total IPSD** | Sum of all inpatient service days during the period (a daily census count) |
| **Available beds** | The bed complement — not licensed, not staffed |
| **Days in period** | Calendar days in the reporting period (30, 31, 90, 365, etc.) |

### The Denominator Is the Key
The denominator — **available beds × days** — represents the *theoretical maximum* of inpatient service days. If every bed was occupied every day, what would the total be? That's your denominator.

### Example Setup (solved in next card)
- Available beds: 320
- Days in period: 90 (one quarter)
- Total IPSD for the quarter: 25,600
- Denominator: 320 × 90 = 28,800
- Result: (25,600 / 28,800) × 100 = **88.9%**`,
