      type: "concept",
      title: "Monthly vs Annual Occupancy",
      content: `## Adjusting for Different Reporting Periods

The occupancy formula is the same whether you're calculating for a single month, a quarter, or a full year — only the **number of days** in the denominator changes.

### Monthly Calculation
Use the exact number of calendar days in that month.

| Month | Days | Denominator (300 beds) |
|-------|------|----------------------|
| January | 31 | 300 × 31 = 9,300 |
| February | 28 | 300 × 28 = 8,400 |
| March | 31 | 300 × 31 = 9,300 |

### Annual Calculation
Use 365 days (or 366 in a leap year).
- 300 beds × 365 = **109,500** possible patient days for the year

### Why Monthly Rates Fluctuate
- Shorter months (February) have fewer possible patient days → rates appear higher with the same census
- Seasonal illness patterns cause census to rise in winter months
- **Annual rates smooth out these fluctuations** and give the clearest long-term picture

### Reporting Best Practice
Always label which period your rate covers:
- "March occupancy: 84%"
- "Q2 occupancy: 81%"
- "FY2024 annual occupancy: 82%"

Never report an occupancy rate without specifying the time period.`,
