      type: "concept",
      title: "Occupancy Benchmarks",
      content: `## What Does the Number Mean?

Calculating an occupancy rate is only the beginning. The real value comes from interpreting that number in context.

### Industry Benchmarks

| Rate | Interpretation |
|------|---------------|
| **Below 75%** | Potential underutilization — beds are sitting empty, fixed costs are not being covered |
| **75–80%** | Acceptable but below optimal — room to grow admissions |
| **80–85%** | **Ideal range** — efficient use of capacity with buffer for surges |
| **85–90%** | Approaching high utilization — monitor staffing closely |
| **Above 90%** | High strain — patient safety risk, staff burnout, admissions may need to be diverted |

### Why 80–85% Is Ideal
- Leaves approximately 15–20% of beds available to handle unexpected admission surges (flu season, mass casualty, etc.)
- Sufficient volume to cover fixed overhead and generate margin
- Staffing ratios remain manageable

### Context Matters
Benchmarks vary by:
- **Hospital type** (critical access hospitals in rural areas may run lower)
- **Unit type** (ICU benchmarks differ from med/surg)
- **Payer mix and seasonality**
- **COVID-era context** (nationally, rates dropped dramatically in 2020–2021)

HIM professionals report the rate — clinical and administrative leaders interpret and act on it.`,
