      type: "quiz",
      title: "Quiz: Occupancy Formula",
      content: `## Quiz Question

A hospital has **280 available beds**. During the month of **October (31 days)**, the hospital recorded **7,336 inpatient service days**.

**What is the correct denominator to use in the occupancy rate formula?**

---

**A)** 280 — just the number of available beds

**B)** 7,336 — the total inpatient service days

**C)** 280 × 31 = **8,680** — available beds times days in the period

**D)** 280 × 30 = 8,400 — using an average 30-day month

---

### Answer: **C) 8,680**

The denominator is always **available beds × actual calendar days** in the reporting period. October has 31 days, so:

280 × 31 = **8,680**

The occupancy rate is:
(7,336 ÷ 8,680) × 100 = **84.5%**

**Why the others are wrong:**
- **A** ignores the time dimension entirely — the rate would just be a ratio of beds, which makes no sense as a daily figure
- **B** is the numerator, not the denominator
- **D** uses 30 days — always use the actual calendar days in the specific month, never an approximation`,
