      type: "intro",
      title: "Autopsy Rates in Health Care Statistics",
      content: "An **autopsy** (also called a postmortem examination) is a systematic examination of a body after death to determine cause of death, evaluate disease processes, and identify unexpected findings. In the hospital setting, autopsies serve as a critical quality assurance tool — confirming or correcting clinical diagnoses and improving future patient care.\n\nHospital administrators and health information professionals track **autopsy rates** to measure how thoroughly the facility investigates inpatient deaths. These rates appear on facility statistics reports and are used by quality improvement committees, tissue committees, and accreditation bodies.\n\nIn this lesson you will learn:\n- The **purpose** of hospital autopsies\n- How to calculate **gross**, **net**, **newborn**, and **fetal** autopsy rates\n- The difference between a **hospital autopsy** and a **medical examiner autopsy**\n- Which deaths are **reportable** to the coroner or medical examiner — and why they change the denominator\n- National trends in autopsy rates and their quality implications",
