      title: "Example B: Newborn Autopsy Rate",
      content: "**Scenario:**\nDuring the same quarter, the hospital's neonatal unit reported:\n- Total newborn (neonatal) deaths: **18**\n- Newborn autopsies performed by hospital pathologist: **14**\n\n---\n\n**Calculation:**\n\n> **Newborn Autopsy Rate = Newborn Autopsies ÷ Newborn Deaths × 100**\n\n> Newborn Autopsy Rate = 14 ÷ 18 × 100\n\n> = 0.7778 × 100\n\n> **= 77.8%**\n\n---\n\n**Interpretation:**\nThe facility autopsied 14 of 18 newborn deaths, achieving a 77.8% newborn autopsy rate. This is substantially higher than the national average for adult autopsies because:\n- Newborn families often want answers about congenital defects or unexpected death\n- Autopsy findings guide genetic counseling for future pregnancies\n- NICU and delivery teams use findings to improve clinical protocols\n\n---\n\n**Important distinctions:**\n- Newborn deaths are counted **separately** from adult inpatient deaths\n- They do **not** feed into the gross or net autopsy rate for adult inpatients\n- ME/coroner jurisdiction applies to newborns too (e.g., suspected abuse) — same exclusion logic applies if needed\n- Rounding: carry to one decimal place unless instructed otherwise",
