      type: "concept",
      title: "Measures of Variability",
      content: `## Measures of Variability

Central tendency tells us the center; **variability** tells us how **spread out** the data is. Two units can have the same average LOS but very different variability.

### Range
**Range = Maximum − Minimum**

Simple but sensitive to outliers.
*Example: LOS 2–14 days → Range = 12*

### Variance
**Variance (s²)** = average of squared deviations from the mean

- Squaring removes negative signs
- Units are squared (e.g., days²) — less intuitive
- Higher variance = more spread

### Standard Deviation (SD)
**SD (s) = √Variance**

- Same units as original data (days)
- Most commonly reported measure of spread
- **Small SD** = data clusters near mean (consistent care)
- **Large SD** = data widely spread (variable care patterns)

### Why Variability Matters in HIM

| Measure | HIM Use |
|---------|---------|
| Range | Quick spread check |
| Variance | Statistical tests |
| SD | Reporting, benchmarking, quality metrics |`,
