      type: "concept",
      title: "Frequency Distributions",
      content: `## Frequency Distributions

A **frequency distribution** organizes data into classes/categories and shows how often each occurs. It is the foundation for histograms and most statistical summaries.

### Three Types of Frequency

| Column | Formula | Meaning |
|--------|---------|---------|
| **Frequency (f)** | Count of values | Raw count |
| **Relative Frequency** | f / n × 100 | % of total |
| **Cumulative Frequency** | Running total % | "Up to this point" |

### HIM Example: LOS Frequency Table

| LOS (days) | Frequency | Relative Freq | Cumulative Freq |
|-----------|-----------|--------------|----------------|
| 1–2 | 5 | 10% | 10% |
| 3–4 | 12 | 24% | 34% |
| 5–6 | 18 | 36% | 70% |
| 7–8 | 10 | 20% | 90% |
| 9+ | 5 | 10% | 100% |

**n = 50 patients**

### Interpreting the Table
- **Mode class:** 5–6 days (36% of patients)
- **70% of patients** were discharged by day 6
- Only **10%** had extended stays (9+ days)`,
