      type: "intro",
      title: "Why Data Presentation Matters in Healthcare",
      content: `Data presentation is the process of organizing and displaying data in visual or tabular form so that patterns, trends, and relationships become clear to the audience.

In Health Information Management, raw numbers alone rarely communicate meaning. A column of 500 length-of-stay values tells a reader nothing at a glance. A well-constructed histogram or table transforms those numbers into actionable intelligence.

**Why presentation matters in HIM:**
- Medical staff committees review mortality, infection, and readmission data monthly
- Accreditation bodies (TJC, CMS) require performance data displayed in trend format
- Quality improvement teams use run charts to detect process changes
- Coders and billers use frequency tables to spot DRG distribution shifts
- Administrators compare department performance using dashboards and bar charts

**Three main display types:**
1. **Tables** — precise values, multiple variables, comparisons
2. **Charts/graphs** — trends, proportions, distributions, relationships
3. **Dashboards** — combined KPIs for at-a-glance monitoring

**Choosing the right display** depends on:
- What type of data (categorical, continuous, time-series)
- What question you are answering
- Who the audience is

This lesson covers every major chart type, rules for effective display, healthcare-specific applications, and how to avoid misleading presentations.`,
