      title: "Frequency Polygons: Overlaying Distributions",
      content: `A **frequency polygon** is created by connecting the midpoints of each bar in a histogram with straight line segments. It shows the same distribution as a histogram but as a line rather than bars.

**Construction steps:**
1. Build a frequency table with class intervals and frequencies
2. Calculate the midpoint of each class interval
3. Plot a point at (midpoint, frequency) for each class
4. Connect the points with straight lines
5. Close the polygon by connecting the first and last points to the x-axis at zero frequency (one class width before the first bin and one after the last bin)

**Why use a frequency polygon instead of a histogram?**
- You can overlay two or more distributions on the same axes using different line colors — this is extremely useful for comparison
- With a histogram, overlapping bars obscure each other
- The polygon shows the shape of the distribution with less visual clutter

**Healthcare application — comparing two groups:**
- Overlay the LOS distribution for Medicare patients vs. Medicaid patients
- Overlay pre-intervention vs. post-intervention distributions of wait times
- Compare age distributions of two service lines

**Key points:**
- The area under a frequency polygon is the same as the area of the histogram bars
- For relative frequency polygons, the y-axis shows proportions (%) so distributions of different sizes can be fairly compared
- Smooth curves fitted through the polygon approximate the theoretical probability distribution`,
