      title: "Healthcare-Specific Displays: Control Charts and Pareto Charts",
      content: `Healthcare quality improvement uses specialized chart types that go beyond standard statistical graphics.

**Control Chart (Shewhart Chart / Run Chart with Limits):**
A control chart is a line graph with three additional horizontal lines:
- **Center line (CL):** the process mean or median
- **Upper Control Limit (UCL):** mean + 3 standard deviations
- **Lower Control Limit (LCL):** mean − 3 standard deviations

Points outside the control limits signal **special cause variation** — something unusual happened that warrants investigation. Points within the limits represent **common cause variation** — normal process fluctuation.

**Healthcare uses:**
- Monthly CLABSI (central line-associated bloodstream infection) rates
- Daily ED wait times to detect outlier days
- Weekly medication error rates
- Readmission rates by quarter

**Rules for signals (beyond control limits):**
- One point outside UCL or LCL
- Eight consecutive points on the same side of the center line
- Six consecutive points trending in one direction

**Pareto Chart:**
A Pareto chart combines a bar chart and a line graph. Bars show the frequency of each cause, sorted from highest to lowest. The line shows the cumulative percentage.

Based on the **Pareto principle (80/20 rule):** roughly 80% of problems come from 20% of causes.

**Healthcare uses:**
- Causes of medication errors (wrong dose, wrong drug, wrong patient...)
- Reasons for denied claims (missing authorization, invalid code, late submission...)
- Causes of patient complaints
- Sources of coding errors

**Dashboard KPIs:**
Healthcare dashboards display multiple metrics simultaneously using gauges, spark lines, and color-coded indicators (red/yellow/green) for rapid monitoring of key performance indicators.`,
