      type: "concept",
      title: "Data Quality in Presentation: Misleading Charts",
      content: `How data is displayed can dramatically change what the audience concludes — even when the underlying numbers are identical. HIM professionals must recognize and avoid misleading presentations.

**Common ways charts mislead:**

**1. Truncated Y-axis (most common)**
Starting the Y-axis at a value other than zero makes small differences appear enormous. A readmission rate change from 14.8% to 15.2% looks like a massive spike if the axis starts at 14%.
- Rule: Always start the Y-axis at zero for bar charts. For line graphs, truncation may be acceptable if clearly labeled and the context is obvious.

**2. Inconsistent scale intervals**
If the X-axis intervals are unequal (e.g., Jan, Feb, Mar, then jumping to Dec), a trend line will appear distorted.

**3. Dual Y-axes with different scales**
Plotting two variables on the same chart with different scales can make their trends appear to correlate when they may not.

**4. Cherry-picking time periods**
Showing only the months where performance looks best omits the full picture. Always show the complete time period.

**5. Chartjunk (Edward Tufte's concept)**
Unnecessary visual elements — gridlines, decorative images, shadows, 3D effects, gradient fills — that add no information but increase visual clutter. They reduce the data-ink ratio.

**Data-ink ratio (Tufte):**
Data-ink ratio = data ink / total ink used
Maximize this ratio: every mark on the chart should serve a data purpose. Remove gridlines, borders, and backgrounds that do not help the reader.

**6. Manipulated pie charts**
"Exploding" one slice or using 3D perspective makes that slice appear larger than its actual proportion.

**Best practices to ensure honest presentation:**
- Start axes at zero (especially bar charts)
- Use consistent, equal intervals on all axes
- Show complete and relevant time periods
- Minimize non-data ink (gridlines, borders, fills)
- Label all axes clearly with units
- Include sample sizes`,
