      type: "quiz",
      title: "Quiz: Interpreting a Box Plot",
      content: `A box plot of patient wait times (minutes) in the Emergency Department shows: Minimum=8, Q1=22, Median=38, Q3=55, Maximum=74, with outlier dots at 95, 110, and 142 minutes. What is the interquartile range (IQR) and what does it tell you?

A. IQR = 74 − 8 = 66 minutes; this is the full range of wait times
B. IQR = 55 − 22 = 33 minutes; this is the spread of the middle 50% of patients
C. IQR = 142 − 8 = 134 minutes; this includes all values including outliers
D. IQR = 38 − 22 = 16 minutes; this is the distance from Q1 to the median

---

**Answer: B**

**Explanation:**
**IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 55 − 22 = 33 minutes**

The IQR represents the spread of the **middle 50%** of the data — the width of the box in a box plot. It tells you:
- 50% of ED patients waited between 22 and 55 minutes
- The "typical" range of wait times is 33 minutes wide
- The IQR is resistant to outliers (unlike the range), making it a robust measure of spread

**Why the others are wrong:**
- **A:** 74 − 8 = 66 is the **range** (max minus min of non-outlier values), not the IQR
- **C:** 142 − 8 = 134 would include outliers — this is not a standard statistic name
- **D:** Median − Q1 is only half the box width; IQR always spans Q1 to Q3

**Outlier check using IQR:**
- Lower fence: Q1 − 1.5 × IQR = 22 − 49.5 = −27.5 (no lower outliers possible)
- Upper fence: Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 55 + 49.5 = 104.5
- Values at 110 and 142 exceed 104.5 → confirmed outliers (plotted as individual dots)`,
