      type: "application",
      title: "HIM Professional's Role in Vital Statistics",
      content: "Health information management professionals are at the center of vital statistics accuracy and public health reporting. Their responsibilities span the entire lifecycle of vital records data.\n\n**Birth certificate responsibilities**:\n- Coordinate with nursing staff to ensure timely completion within state-required timeframes (typically 5–10 days after delivery)\n- Verify demographic data accuracy (mother, father, infant)\n- Submit electronically through state's Electronic Birth Registration System (EBRS)\n- Correct errors through amendment process with state vital records office\n\n**Death certificate responsibilities**:\n- Assign ICD-10-CM/PCS codes to all causes of death listed on the certificate\n- Identify the underlying cause of death for national mortality coding\n- Communicate with attending physicians when cause of death statements are incomplete, ambiguous, or medically implausible (e.g., 'respiratory failure' alone without an underlying cause)\n- Submit completed certificates to local/county registrar within required timeframe\n\n**Reportable disease responsibilities**:\n- Ensure documentation in the EHR supports identification of reportable conditions\n- Participate in implementation of electronic case reporting (eCR) — automated extraction and submission of case data from the EHR to health departments\n- Maintain privacy protections (HIPAA) while complying with mandatory public health reporting, which is a recognized exception to the minimum necessary standard\n\n**Data quality and integrity**:\n- Monitor completion rates and timeliness metrics\n- Conduct retrospective audits of death certificate coding accuracy\n- Participate in quality improvement initiatives to reduce incomplete or inaccurate vital records",
