      content: `# Why Data Quality Matters

Health information drives decisions at every level — from bedside care to national policy. When data is wrong, the consequences are serious.

**Patient Safety**
- Incorrect allergies or diagnoses in the record can lead to medication errors or missed diagnoses
- Duplicate records may cause providers to treat the wrong patient profile

**Reimbursement**
- Incomplete or inaccurate coding results in claim denials and underpayment
- Payers audit records; poor documentation = lost revenue

**Regulatory Reporting**
- Public health agencies, CMS, and accreditors rely on submitted data
- Inaccurate data skews quality metrics and can trigger audits

**Legal & Compliance**
- The health record is a legal document; deficiencies create liability

> "Garbage in, garbage out" — data quality begins at the point of entry and must be maintained throughout the record lifecycle.`,
