      type: "concept",
      title: "Data Audits & Monitoring",
      content: `# Data Audits & Monitoring

Audits are systematic reviews of records to measure data quality and compliance.

## Types of Audits

**Retrospective Audit**
- Performed *after* the patient is discharged
- Reviews completed records for deficiencies
- Most common type in HIM
- Example: Monthly review of 30 discharged records for completeness

**Concurrent Audit**
- Performed *while the patient is still admitted*
- Allows real-time corrections before discharge
- Supports CDI and timely coding
- Example: Reviewing H&P within 24 hours of admission

**Prospective Audit**
- Reviews processes *before* records are created
- Less common; used for new workflow or EHR implementation

## What Gets Monitored
- Deficiency rates by record type and physician
- Delinquency rates (records past due)
- Coding accuracy rates
- Query rates (CDI queries to physicians)

## Audit Documentation
- Results tracked in audit log
- Reported to HIM director and medical staff committee
- Trends used to drive education and process improvement

> Regular auditing creates a culture of quality — problems are caught early, not during a TJC survey.`,
