      type: "concept",
      title: "Data Governance",
      content: `# Data Governance

**Data governance** is the framework of policies, standards, and roles that ensures health data is managed consistently, securely, and with accountability.

## Key Components

**Policies & Standards**
- Define what data must be collected, how it is formatted, and how long it is retained
- Data dictionaries standardize terminology across systems

**Data Stewardship**
- A **data steward** is responsible for a specific data domain
- Ensures accuracy, accessibility, and security of their data area
- Example: HIM director as steward of the health record

**Data Ownership**
- **Data owner**: Senior leader accountable for a data set (e.g., CNO owns nursing documentation standards)
- **Data custodian**: IT department that manages storage and access

**Data Quality Committee / Governance Board**
- Cross-functional team (HIM, IT, clinical, compliance, administration)
- Sets data standards and resolves data quality disputes
- Reviews audit results and drives improvement initiatives

**Why It Matters**
- Without governance, data quality degrades over time
- Ensures compliance with HIPAA, CMS, and accreditation standards
- Supports analytics and AI reliability

> Good governance turns data into a trusted organizational asset.`,
