      type: "concept",
      title: "Vital Statistics: Birth & Death Rates",
      content: `# Vital Statistics — Birth & Death Rates

These rates describe the **general population**, not just hospital patients. Multiplier is **1,000** for most.

## Birth Rate
**Formula:**
\`\`\`
Birth Rate = (Live Births / Midyear Population) × 1,000
\`\`\`
**Mnemonic:** "Births per thousand neighbors" — live births over the whole community

---

## Crude Death Rate
**Formula:**
\`\`\`
Crude Death Rate = (Total Deaths / Midyear Population) × 1,000
\`\`\`
**Mnemonic:** "Crude = all Corpses over Community × 1,000"

---

## Cause-Specific Death Rate
**Formula:**
\`\`\`
Cause-Specific Death Rate = (Deaths from Specific Cause / Midyear Population) × 100,000
\`\`\`
**Mnemonic:** "Cause-Specific = Counts one disease × 100K (rarer event needs bigger multiplier)"

---

## Why "Midyear Population"?
Population changes throughout the year (births, deaths, migration). The midyear estimate (July 1) represents the average population "at risk" for the whole year.

---

## Worked Example
City population July 1: 50,000. Births: 600. Total deaths: 400. Cancer deaths: 80.

**Birth Rate:** (600/50,000) × 1,000 = **12.0 per 1,000**
**Crude Death Rate:** (400/50,000) × 1,000 = **8.0 per 1,000**
**Cancer Death Rate:** (80/50,000) × 100,000 = **160.0 per 100,000**

---

## Multiplier Rule
- Common events (births, crude deaths) → **× 1,000**
- Rare events (specific causes, maternal) → **× 100,000**`,
