            content: "The **21st Century Cures Act (2016)** is a landmark law that transformed health information exchange. The RHIT exam tests several key provisions:\n\n**Information Blocking:**\n• Defined as practices that **unreasonably prevent, materially discourage, or interfere** with the access, exchange, or use of electronic health information (EHI)\n• Applies to **healthcare providers, health IT developers, HIEs, and HINs**\n• Violations can result in penalties up to **$1 million per violation** for IT developers\n\n**8 Exceptions to Information Blocking** (you don't need to memorize all 8 names, but know the concept):\n• Preventing harm, privacy, security, infeasibility, health IT performance, content/manner, fees, licensing\n\n**TEFCA (Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement):**\n• National framework for nationwide health information exchange\n• Establishes **Qualified Health Information Networks (QHINs)**\n• Goal: any provider can share data with any other provider through a standardized trust framework\n\n**ONC (Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT):**\n• Federal entity that sets standards and certifies health IT\n• Oversees the **Promoting Interoperability** program (formerly Meaningful Use)\n• Three stages evolved from MU Stage 1 (2011) through current PI requirements",
