            content: "In 1999, the Institute of Medicine published a report that shocked the nation: **\"To Err Is Human\"** estimated that **44,000 to 98,000 Americans die each year** from preventable medical errors. That is more than car accidents, breast cancer, or AIDS.\n\nThis single publication launched the modern **quality improvement movement** in healthcare. Two years later, the IOM followed up with **\"Crossing the Quality Chasm\"**, which identified six aims for improvement known as **STEEEP** -- a mnemonic you MUST memorize for the RHIT exam.\n\nToday, quality is not optional. CMS ties **hospital reimbursement** to quality performance. The Joint Commission requires continuous quality monitoring. HIM professionals play a central role through **data collection, analysis, clinical documentation improvement (CDI), and reporting**.\n\nThis lesson covers the IOM reports, Donabedian's model, PI tools (PDCA, RCA, FMEA, fishbone, Pareto), CMS quality programs (VBP, HRRP, HAC), and CDI metrics. These concepts are tested heavily in Domain 3.",
