            content: "The RHIT exam distinguishes between two fundamental research approaches:\n\n**Quantitative Research** (numbers-based):\n• Uses **numerical data** and statistical analysis\n• Tests hypotheses, measures relationships, determines cause and effect\n• Results are **generalizable** to larger populations\n• Three types:\n  - **Experimental** -- Researcher CONTROLS variables (RCT is the gold standard)\n  - **Quasi-experimental** -- Some control, but NO randomization\n  - **Descriptive** -- Observes and describes without manipulation (surveys, chart reviews)\n\n**Qualitative Research** (meaning-based):\n• Explores **experiences, meanings, and perceptions** through words, not numbers\n• Uses interviews, focus groups, observation\n• Results provide DEPTH but are NOT generalizable\n• Three approaches:\n  - **Phenomenology** -- Explores the LIVED EXPERIENCE of participants\n  - **Ethnography** -- Studies CULTURES and social groups\n  - **Grounded Theory** -- Develops a THEORY from the data (data-driven, not hypothesis-driven)\n\n**Exam Key:** Quantitative = numbers, hypothesis testing, generalizable. Qualitative = words, exploration, depth. If a question mentions interviews about patient experiences, it's qualitative.",
