                                                  type:  "application",
                                                  title:  "Study Guide: Vocabularies & Classifications",
                                                  content:  "Your RHIT exam study checklist for clinical vocabularies and classifications:\n\n**Core Distinctions:**\n• Classification = grouping for statistics/billing (ICD-10-CM)\n• Nomenclature = systematic naming with clinical detail (SNOMED CT)\n• Terminology = broadest term, umbrella for both\n\n**Master This Table:**\n• ICD-10-CM: Diagnosis, NCHS, Oct 1, 3-7 chars\n• ICD-10-PCS: Inpatient procedures, CMS, Oct 1, 7 chars, 31 root operations\n• CPT: Physician/outpatient, AMA, Jan 1, 5 digits, 3 categories\n• HCPCS II: Supplies/DME, CMS, Letter+4 digits\n• SNOMED CT: Clinical concepts, SNOMED International/NLM, 350K+ concepts\n• LOINC: Labs/observations, Regenstrief Institute, 90K+ codes\n• RxNorm: Medications, NLM\n• CDT: Dental, ADA, D+4 digits\n• DSM-5: Mental health criteria, APA (uses ICD-10-CM codes for billing)\n• NDC: Drug products, FDA, 10 digits\n• ICD-O-3: Oncology, topography + morphology\n\n**UMLS (NLM):** Metathesaurus (links vocabularies), Semantic Network (categorizes), SPECIALIST Lexicon (NLP)\n\n**GEMs:** ICD-9 to ICD-10 mappings, NOT one-to-one, CMS+NCHS\n\n**Key Traps:**\n• CPT updates Jan 1, ICD updates Oct 1\n• NCHS maintains CM, CMS maintains PCS\n• Excision = partial, Resection = total\n• Category II CPT = NOT for billing\n• DSM-5 provides criteria, NOT codes",
